{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LPECfPWkieoKb9r8pmafwJ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n2\t\n3\t**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KP(1/4)\n4\t**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** PUTIH / WHITE\n5\t\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## Work Activity 1: Conduct BEV Daily Briefing\n9\t\n10\t## Objektif Pembelajaran\n11\t1. Explain small group communication techniques for BEV workshop briefings\n12\t2. Describe work activities briefing content and procedures\n13\t3. Explain the daily briefing feedback/response reporting process\n14\t\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## 1. BEV Small Group Communication (Komunikasi Kumpulan Kecil)\n18\t\n19\t### 1.1 Communication Techniques\n20\tEffective daily briefing requires:\n21\t- **Clarity:** Use simple, direct language. Technical terms should be explained.\n22\t- **Brevity:** Keep briefings concise (15-20 minutes maximum).\n23\t- **Structure:** Follow a consistent format so team members know what to expect.\n24\t- **Engagement:** Ask questions, invite input, observe body language for understanding.\n25\t- **Visual aids:** Use the job control board, vehicle service boards, or whiteboards to display information.\n26\t\n27\t### 1.2 Briefing Content Structure\n28\tA standard daily briefing should cover:\n29\t1. **Safety reminder** — safety topic of the day, recent incidents/near misses\n30\t2. **Work progress update** — status of vehicles currently in workshop\n31\t3. **New assignments** — vehicles arriving today, job allocation\n32\t4. **Service information updates** — manufacturer technical bulletins, new procedures\n33\t5. **Housekeeping** — 5S compliance, workshop cleanliness\n34\t6. **Questions and feedback** — open floor for team input\n35\t\n36\t### 1.3 Procedure\n37\t1. Schedule briefing at start of shift (consistent time daily)\n38\t2. Gather all technicians at designated briefing area\n39\t3. Take attendance using attendance list\n40\t4. Deliver briefing using prepared notes/agenda\n41\t5. Invite questions and address concerns\n42\t6. Distribute job cards/work orders\n43\t\n44\t### 1.4 Attendance List\n45\t- Maintain a signed attendance list for every briefing\n46\t- Records demonstrate compliance with safety communication requirements\n47\t- Attendance records are auditable documents for quality management systems\n48\t\n49\t---\n50\t\n51\t## 2. Work Activities Briefing (Taklimat Aktiviti Kerja)\n52\t\n53\t### 2.1 Key Content Areas\n54\t\n55\t**Safety Requirements:**\n56\t- HV safety reminders specific to today's jobs\n57\t- PPE requirements for each task\n58\t- Any special safety precautions (e.g., battery pack removal today — 3-person minimum)\n59\t- Review of any recent safety incidents or near-miss reports\n60\t\n61\t**Environment Requirements:**\n62\t- Waste management reminders\n63\t- Coolant and refrigerant handling procedures\n64\t- Environmental compliance requirements\n65\t\n66\t**Work Progress:**\n67\t- Status of vehicles in progress from previous shift/day\n68\t- Expected completion times\n69\t- Any technical challenges or parts on order\n70\t\n71\t**New Assignments:**\n72\t- New vehicles received or scheduled today\n73\t- Task allocation to technicians (matching skill level to job complexity)\n74\t- Expected time for each job\n75\t\n76\t**Service Information Updates:**\n77\t- New manufacturer Technical Service Bulletins (TSBs)\n78\t- Updated procedures or safety notices\n79\t- Recall information affecting vehicles in workshop\n80\t\n81\t---\n82\t\n83\t## 3. Daily Briefing Feedback/Response Report\n84\t\n85\t### 3.1 Update Job Control Board\n86\tAfter the briefing:\n87\t- Update the workshop job control board (papan kawalan kerja) with:\n88\t  - Vehicle registration numbers and job status\n89\t  - Technician assignments\n90\t  - Expected completion times\n91\t  - Parts status (ordered, received, pending)\n92\t\n93\t### 3.2 Follow Up Work Status\n94\t- During the day, the supervisor monitors progress against the briefing plan\n95\t- Identify delays early and reallocate resources if needed\n96\t- End-of-day: update job control board with actual status\n97\t- Prepare notes for the next day's briefing based on the day's outcomes\n98\t\n99\t### 3.3 Report Documentation\n100\t- Briefing notes should be filed (hardcopy and/or softcopy)\n101\t- Any safety concerns raised during briefing must be documented and followed up\n102\t- Feedback from team members should be recorded and actioned\n103\t\n104\t---\n105\t\n106\t## Rujukan\n107\t1. Company SOP for daily briefing\n108\t2. OSHA 1994 (Act 514) — employer communication responsibilities\n109\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"045710be-d4d0-4840-be77-82fe81964441","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:18:08.649Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-01.md","content":"# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KP(1/4)\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** PUTIH / WHITE\n\n---\n\n## Work Activity 1: Conduct BEV Daily Briefing\n\n## Objektif Pembelajaran\n1. Explain small group communication techniques for BEV workshop briefings\n2. Describe work activities briefing content and procedures\n3. Explain the daily briefing feedback/response reporting process\n\n---\n\n## 1. BEV Small Group Communication (Komunikasi Kumpulan Kecil)\n\n### 1.1 Communication Techniques\nEffective daily briefing requires:\n- **Clarity:** Use simple, direct language. Technical terms should be explained.\n- **Brevity:** Keep briefings concise (15-20 minutes maximum).\n- **Structure:** Follow a consistent format so team members know what to expect.\n- **Engagement:** Ask questions, invite input, observe body language for understanding.\n- **Visual aids:** Use the job control board, vehicle service boards, or whiteboards to display information.\n\n### 1.2 Briefing Content Structure\nA standard daily briefing should cover:\n1. **Safety reminder** — safety topic of the day, recent incidents/near misses\n2. **Work progress update** — status of vehicles currently in workshop\n3. **New assignments** — vehicles arriving today, job allocation\n4. **Service information updates** — manufacturer technical bulletins, new procedures\n5. **Housekeeping** — 5S compliance, workshop cleanliness\n6. **Questions and feedback** — open floor for team input\n\n### 1.3 Procedure\n1. Schedule briefing at start of shift (consistent time daily)\n2. Gather all technicians at designated briefing area\n3. Take attendance using attendance list\n4. Deliver briefing using prepared notes/agenda\n5. Invite questions and address concerns\n6. Distribute job cards/work orders\n\n### 1.4 Attendance List\n- Maintain a signed attendance list for every briefing\n- Records demonstrate compliance with safety communication requirements\n- Attendance records are auditable documents for quality management systems\n\n---\n\n## 2. Work Activities Briefing (Taklimat Aktiviti Kerja)\n\n### 2.1 Key Content Areas\n\n**Safety Requirements:**\n- HV safety reminders specific to today's jobs\n- PPE requirements for each task\n- Any special safety precautions (e.g., battery pack removal today — 3-person minimum)\n- Review of any recent safety incidents or near-miss reports\n\n**Environment Requirements:**\n- Waste management reminders\n- Coolant and refrigerant handling procedures\n- Environmental compliance requirements\n\n**Work Progress:**\n- Status of vehicles in progress from previous shift/day\n- Expected completion times\n- Any technical challenges or parts on order\n\n**New Assignments:**\n- New vehicles received or scheduled today\n- Task allocation to technicians (matching skill level to job complexity)\n- Expected time for each job\n\n**Service Information Updates:**\n- New manufacturer Technical Service Bulletins (TSBs)\n- Updated procedures or safety notices\n- Recall information affecting vehicles in workshop\n\n---\n\n## 3. Daily Briefing Feedback/Response Report\n\n### 3.1 Update Job Control Board\nAfter the briefing:\n- Update the workshop job control board (papan kawalan kerja) with:\n  - Vehicle registration numbers and job status\n  - Technician assignments\n  - Expected completion times\n  - Parts status (ordered, received, pending)\n\n### 3.2 Follow Up Work Status\n- During the day, the supervisor monitors progress against the briefing plan\n- Identify delays early and reallocate resources if needed\n- End-of-day: update job control board with actual status\n- Prepare notes for the next day's briefing based on the day's outcomes\n\n### 3.3 Report Documentation\n- Briefing notes should be filed (hardcopy and/or softcopy)\n- Any safety concerns raised during briefing must be documented and followed up\n- Feedback from team members should be recorded and actioned\n\n---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. Company SOP for daily briefing\n2. OSHA 1994 (Act 514) — employer communication responsibilities\n","numLines":109,"startLine":1,"totalLines":109}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_017nPDNDVgZtiqSCuGioS2Nx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HHD92xYEVaLstjgvjEkDUc","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-02.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":204,"cache_read_input_tokens":30189,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":204},"output_tokens":60,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"03d86ed8-ace1-487b-a15d-16011f677f3b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HHD92xYEVaLstjgvjEkDUc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n2\t\n3\t**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KP(2/4)\n4\t**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** PUTIH / WHITE\n5\t\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## Work Activity 2: Monitor BEV Subordinate's Performance\n9\t\n10\t## Objektif Pembelajaran\n11\t1. Explain subordinate performance appraisal methods and formats\n12\t2. Describe performance evaluation criteria for BEV technicians\n13\t3. Explain performance improvement techniques\n14\t4. Describe the performance reporting process\n15\t\n16\t---\n17\t\n18\t## 1. Subordinate Performance Appraisal Method (Kaedah Penilaian Prestasi)\n19\t\n20\t### 1.1 Appraisal Format\n21\t- **Standard evaluation form:** Company-specific form with rating scales (e.g., 1-5 or Excellent/Good/Satisfactory/Needs Improvement)\n22\t- **Contents:** Technical competency, work quality, productivity, safety compliance, teamwork, communication, initiative\n23\t- **Communication channels:** Hardcopy (filed in personnel records) and softcopy (digital HR system)\n24\t\n25\t### 1.2 Appraisal Frequency\n26\t- Formal appraisal: Annually or semi-annually\n27\t- Informal check-ins: Monthly or as needed\n28\t- Probation review: At 3 months and 6 months for new employees\n29\t\n30\t---\n31\t\n32\t## 2. Performance Evaluation Criteria (Kriteria Penilaian Prestasi)\n33\t\n34\t### 2.1 Evaluation Dimensions\n35\t\n36\t| Dimension | What to Assess | BEV-Specific Examples |\n37\t|-----------|---------------|----------------------|\n38\t| **Attitude** | Work ethic, initiative, responsibility | Compliance with HV safety protocols without prompting |\n39\t| **Skills** | Technical competency, problem-solving | Ability to perform HV de-energisation correctly, diagnostic proficiency |\n40\t| **Knowledge** | Theoretical understanding | Understanding of BEV systems, fault code interpretation |\n41\t| **Productivity & Efficiency** | Job completion rate, time management | Number of vehicles serviced per week, time per standard job |\n42\t\n43\t### 2.2 BEV-Specific Competency Assessment\n44\t- Can the technician independently perform HV de-energisation?\n45\t- Can they interpret BEV diagnostic fault codes?\n46\t- Do they consistently follow safety procedures?\n47\t- Can they handle BEV-specific tools and equipment properly?\n48\t\n49\t---\n50\t\n51\t## 3. Performance Improvement Techniques (Teknik Peningkatan Prestasi)\n52\t\n53\t| Technique | Application | When to Use |\n54\t|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n55\t| **On-the-Job Training (OJT)** | Supervised hands-on practice with experienced technician | Skill gaps in specific procedures |\n56\t| **Refresher Course** | Formal classroom/practical refresher on BEV systems | Knowledge decay, new technology introduction |\n57\t| **Counselling** | One-on-one discussion about performance issues | Attitude, behaviour, or persistent performance problems |\n58\t| **Mentoring** | Pairing with senior technician for ongoing guidance | New employees, career development |\n59\t\n60\t---\n61\t\n62\t## 4. Subordinate's Performance Report\n63\t\n64\t### 4.1 Report Forms\n65\t| Form | Purpose |\n66\t|------|---------|\n67\t| Evaluation form | Formal performance rating with scores and comments |\n68\t| Counselling form | Documentation of counselling sessions and agreed actions |\n69\t| Training nomination form | Recommendation for specific training to address skill gaps |\n70\t\n71\t### 4.2 Reporting to Superior\n72\t- Compile performance data for all subordinates\n73\t- Highlight top performers and those requiring intervention\n74\t- Present recommendations: promotions, training, counselling, disciplinary action\n75\t- Report submitted to superior per company policy timeline\n76\t\n77\t---\n78\t\n79\t## Rujukan\n80\t1. Company Human Resource policies\n81\t2. Company performance evaluation SOP\n82\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"0eadbb3f-c496-444d-8f8e-e75b509f815d","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:18:09.086Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-02.md","content":"# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KP(2/4)\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** PUTIH / WHITE\n\n---\n\n## Work Activity 2: Monitor BEV Subordinate's Performance\n\n## Objektif Pembelajaran\n1. Explain subordinate performance appraisal methods and formats\n2. Describe performance evaluation criteria for BEV technicians\n3. Explain performance improvement techniques\n4. Describe the performance reporting process\n\n---\n\n## 1. Subordinate Performance Appraisal Method (Kaedah Penilaian Prestasi)\n\n### 1.1 Appraisal Format\n- **Standard evaluation form:** Company-specific form with rating scales (e.g., 1-5 or Excellent/Good/Satisfactory/Needs Improvement)\n- **Contents:** Technical competency, work quality, productivity, safety compliance, teamwork, communication, initiative\n- **Communication channels:** Hardcopy (filed in personnel records) and softcopy (digital HR system)\n\n### 1.2 Appraisal Frequency\n- Formal appraisal: Annually or semi-annually\n- Informal check-ins: Monthly or as needed\n- Probation review: At 3 months and 6 months for new employees\n\n---\n\n## 2. Performance Evaluation Criteria (Kriteria Penilaian Prestasi)\n\n### 2.1 Evaluation Dimensions\n\n| Dimension | What to Assess | BEV-Specific Examples |\n|-----------|---------------|----------------------|\n| **Attitude** | Work ethic, initiative, responsibility | Compliance with HV safety protocols without prompting |\n| **Skills** | Technical competency, problem-solving | Ability to perform HV de-energisation correctly, diagnostic proficiency |\n| **Knowledge** | Theoretical understanding | Understanding of BEV systems, fault code interpretation |\n| **Productivity & Efficiency** | Job completion rate, time management | Number of vehicles serviced per week, time per standard job |\n\n### 2.2 BEV-Specific Competency Assessment\n- Can the technician independently perform HV de-energisation?\n- Can they interpret BEV diagnostic fault codes?\n- Do they consistently follow safety procedures?\n- Can they handle BEV-specific tools and equipment properly?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Performance Improvement Techniques (Teknik Peningkatan Prestasi)\n\n| Technique | Application | When to Use |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| **On-the-Job Training (OJT)** | Supervised hands-on practice with experienced technician | Skill gaps in specific procedures |\n| **Refresher Course** | Formal classroom/practical refresher on BEV systems | Knowledge decay, new technology introduction |\n| **Counselling** | One-on-one discussion about performance issues | Attitude, behaviour, or persistent performance problems |\n| **Mentoring** | Pairing with senior technician for ongoing guidance | New employees, career development |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Subordinate's Performance Report\n\n### 4.1 Report Forms\n| Form | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| Evaluation form | Formal performance rating with scores and comments |\n| Counselling form | Documentation of counselling sessions and agreed actions |\n| Training nomination form | Recommendation for specific training to address skill gaps |\n\n### 4.2 Reporting to Superior\n- Compile performance data for all subordinates\n- Highlight top performers and those requiring intervention\n- Present recommendations: promotions, training, counselling, disciplinary action\n- Report submitted to superior per company policy timeline\n\n---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. Company Human Resource policies\n2. Company performance evaluation SOP\n","numLines":82,"startLine":1,"totalLines":82}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01GL4qesL9RCpUmxSuzmtzqZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019PikBcprbSK1NERn58mKzw","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-03.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2524,"cache_read_input_tokens":30393,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2524},"output_tokens":40,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"ecd0b159-ef48-4191-bd12-1b88d4a7bc05"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019PikBcprbSK1NERn58mKzw","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n2\t\n3\t**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KP(3/4)\n4\t**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** PUTIH / WHITE\n5\t\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## Work Activity 3: Coordinate BEV Subordinate's Training\n9\t\n10\t## Objektif Pembelajaran\n11\t1. Explain Training Needs Identification (TNI) for BEV technicians\n12\t2. Describe Training Needs Analysis (TNA) methodology\n13\t3. Explain the process for arranging subordinate training\n14\t\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## 1. BEV Training Needs Identification (TNI) — Pengenalpastian Keperluan Latihan\n18\t\n19\t### 1.1 BEV-Related Training Programs\n20\tThe rapid evolution of BEV technology requires continuous training. Key training areas:\n21\t- HV safety certification and recertification\n22\t- New BEV model familiarisation\n23\t- Diagnostic tool software updates and new features\n24\t- Battery technology advances (solid-state, LFP, NMC variants)\n25\t- ADAS (Advanced Driver Assistance Systems) integration with BEV\n26\t- Environmental regulations updates\n27\t\n28\t### 1.2 Skills Chart (Carta Kemahiran)\n29\tMaintain a skills chart for each technician mapping:\n30\t- Current certifications held (NOSS levels, manufacturer certifications)\n31\t- Skills demonstrated (HV work, diagnostics, specific component replacement)\n32\t- Skills gaps identified\n33\t- Training completed and planned\n34\t\n35\t### 1.3 BEV Service Information Updates\n36\tManufacturers regularly release:\n37\t- Technical Service Bulletins (TSBs) requiring new knowledge\n38\t- Safety recalls requiring specific training\n39\t- New model introduction training\n40\t- Updated service procedures\n41\t\n42\t---\n43\t\n44\t## 2. BEV Training Needs Analysis (TNA) — Analisis Keperluan Latihan\n45\t\n46\t### 2.1 Skills Matrix Chart\n47\tA skills matrix maps each technician against required competencies:\n48\t\n49\t| Competency | Tech A | Tech B | Tech C |\n50\t|-----------|--------|--------|--------|\n51\t| HV de-energisation | Y | Y | N |\n52\t| Battery pack removal | Y | N | N |\n53\t| OBC replacement | Y | Y | N |\n54\t| Inverter replacement | N | N | N |\n55\t| HVAC system service | Y | Y | Y |\n56\t| Brake system service | Y | Y | N |\n57\t\n58\tY = Competent, N = Training needed\n59\t\n60\t### 2.2 TNA Form\n61\tThe TNA form documents:\n62\t- Current competency level per technician\n63\t- Required competency level for their role\n64\t- Gap between current and required\n65\t- Priority ranking (critical vs. desirable)\n66\t- Recommended training intervention\n67\t\n68\t### 2.3 Training Program Availability\n69\tSurvey available training:\n70\t- JPK-accredited training centres (Pusat Bertauliah)\n71\t- Manufacturer training academies (e.g., Tesla, BYD, Mercedes training centres)\n72\t- Industry associations (MAA, PEKEMA)\n73\t- Online/e-learning platforms\n74\t- In-house training by senior staff\n75\t\n76\t---\n77\t\n78\t## 3. Arrange Subordinate's Training (Pengurusan Latihan)\n79\t\n80\t### 3.1 Training Plan (TP)\n81\tDevelop an annual training plan:\n82\t- List all required training for each technician\n83\t- Schedule by quarter to minimise operational disruption\n84\t- Budget allocation per training\n85\t- Approval process (supervisor, management)\n86\t\n87\t### 3.2 Training Logistics\n88\t| Item | Action |\n89\t|------|--------|\n90\t| Training location | Confirm venue (in-house, external centre, online) |\n91\t| Training provider | Confirm accredited provider, instructor qualifications |\n92\t| Training notification | Issue notification to trainee minimum 2 weeks in advance |\n93\t| Work coverage | Arrange work coverage during trainee's absence |\n94\t| Materials and facilities | Confirm training materials, tools, equipment availability |\n95\t\n96\t### 3.3 Post-Training\n97\t- Collect training certificate/completion record\n98\t- Update skills chart and personnel file\n99\t- Evaluate training effectiveness (can the technician now perform the skill?)\n100\t- Share key learnings with team during daily briefing\n101\t\n102\t---\n103\t\n104\t## Rujukan\n105\t1. Company training policy\n106\t2. JPK/DSD training guidelines\n107\t3. Manufacturer training catalogues\n108\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"86642dc8-889b-440d-980d-78191a7ba15c","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:18:12.189Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-03.md","content":"# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KP(3/4)\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** PUTIH / WHITE\n\n---\n\n## Work Activity 3: Coordinate BEV Subordinate's Training\n\n## Objektif Pembelajaran\n1. Explain Training Needs Identification (TNI) for BEV technicians\n2. Describe Training Needs Analysis (TNA) methodology\n3. Explain the process for arranging subordinate training\n\n---\n\n## 1. BEV Training Needs Identification (TNI) — Pengenalpastian Keperluan Latihan\n\n### 1.1 BEV-Related Training Programs\nThe rapid evolution of BEV technology requires continuous training. Key training areas:\n- HV safety certification and recertification\n- New BEV model familiarisation\n- Diagnostic tool software updates and new features\n- Battery technology advances (solid-state, LFP, NMC variants)\n- ADAS (Advanced Driver Assistance Systems) integration with BEV\n- Environmental regulations updates\n\n### 1.2 Skills Chart (Carta Kemahiran)\nMaintain a skills chart for each technician mapping:\n- Current certifications held (NOSS levels, manufacturer certifications)\n- Skills demonstrated (HV work, diagnostics, specific component replacement)\n- Skills gaps identified\n- Training completed and planned\n\n### 1.3 BEV Service Information Updates\nManufacturers regularly release:\n- Technical Service Bulletins (TSBs) requiring new knowledge\n- Safety recalls requiring specific training\n- New model introduction training\n- Updated service procedures\n\n---\n\n## 2. BEV Training Needs Analysis (TNA) — Analisis Keperluan Latihan\n\n### 2.1 Skills Matrix Chart\nA skills matrix maps each technician against required competencies:\n\n| Competency | Tech A | Tech B | Tech C |\n|-----------|--------|--------|--------|\n| HV de-energisation | Y | Y | N |\n| Battery pack removal | Y | N | N |\n| OBC replacement | Y | Y | N |\n| Inverter replacement | N | N | N |\n| HVAC system service | Y | Y | Y |\n| Brake system service | Y | Y | N |\n\nY = Competent, N = Training needed\n\n### 2.2 TNA Form\nThe TNA form documents:\n- Current competency level per technician\n- Required competency level for their role\n- Gap between current and required\n- Priority ranking (critical vs. desirable)\n- Recommended training intervention\n\n### 2.3 Training Program Availability\nSurvey available training:\n- JPK-accredited training centres (Pusat Bertauliah)\n- Manufacturer training academies (e.g., Tesla, BYD, Mercedes training centres)\n- Industry associations (MAA, PEKEMA)\n- Online/e-learning platforms\n- In-house training by senior staff\n\n---\n\n## 3. Arrange Subordinate's Training (Pengurusan Latihan)\n\n### 3.1 Training Plan (TP)\nDevelop an annual training plan:\n- List all required training for each technician\n- Schedule by quarter to minimise operational disruption\n- Budget allocation per training\n- Approval process (supervisor, management)\n\n### 3.2 Training Logistics\n| Item | Action |\n|------|--------|\n| Training location | Confirm venue (in-house, external centre, online) |\n| Training provider | Confirm accredited provider, instructor qualifications |\n| Training notification | Issue notification to trainee minimum 2 weeks in advance |\n| Work coverage | Arrange work coverage during trainee's absence |\n| Materials and facilities | Confirm training materials, tools, equipment availability |\n\n### 3.3 Post-Training\n- Collect training certificate/completion record\n- Update skills chart and personnel file\n- Evaluate training effectiveness (can the technician now perform the skill?)\n- Share key learnings with team during daily briefing\n\n---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. Company training policy\n2. JPK/DSD training guidelines\n3. Manufacturer training catalogues\n","numLines":108,"startLine":1,"totalLines":108}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01GL4qesL9RCpUmxSuzmtzqZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019qSLZhWhDw63SQBERnbkY7","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-04.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2524,"cache_read_input_tokens":30393,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2524},"output_tokens":40,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"37bd355a-d7d6-4247-8043-f4165eff7f3c"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019qSLZhWhDw63SQBERnbkY7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n2\t\n3\t**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KP(4/4)\n4\t**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** PUTIH / WHITE\n5\t\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## Work Activity 4: Monitor BEV Workshop Safety, Health & Environment\n9\t\n10\t## Objektif Pembelajaran\n11\t1. Explain BEV workshop safety, health, and environment responsibilities\n12\t2. Describe BEV-specific safety, health, and environment awareness requirements\n13\t3. Explain the SHE finding report format and reporting process\n14\t\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## 1. BEV Safety, Health, and Environment (Keselamatan, Kesihatan dan Alam Sekitar)\n18\t\n19\t### 1.1 Employer and Employee Responsibilities\n20\t\n21\t**Employer responsibilities (under OSHA 1994):**\n22\t- Provide a safe workplace with appropriate HV safety equipment\n23\t- Ensure all BEV technicians are properly trained and certified\n24\t- Maintain safety equipment in serviceable condition\n25\t- Provide PPE at no cost to employees\n26\t- Establish and enforce safety procedures\n27\t- Report workplace accidents and dangerous occurrences\n28\t\n29\t**Employee responsibilities:**\n30\t- Follow established safety procedures\n31\t- Wear prescribed PPE at all times\n32\t- Report hazards, incidents, and near-misses immediately\n33\t- Not tamper with or disable safety equipment\n34\t- Participate in safety training and briefings\n35\t\n36\t### 1.2 Workplace Safety and Health\n37\tThe supervisor must monitor:\n38\t- HV safety procedures are followed for every BEV job\n39\t- PPE is worn correctly and consistently\n40\t- Tools and equipment are in serviceable condition\n41\t- Work permits are obtained for HV work\n42\t- Two-person rule is observed for HV work\n43\t- Emergency equipment is accessible and maintained\n44\t\n45\t### 1.3 Waste Management Monitoring\n46\tThe supervisor ensures:\n47\t- Scheduled waste is properly classified, contained, and labeled\n48\t- Waste disposal records are maintained\n49\t- Licensed waste contractors are used\n50\t- DOE reporting requirements are met\n51\t- Waste storage areas are compliant\n52\t\n53\t---\n54\t\n55\t## 2. BEV Safety, Health, and Environment Awareness\n56\t\n57\t### 2.1 Basics of Safety, Health, and Environment\n58\t\n59\t**Proper BEV PPE:**\n60\t- Monitor that all technicians have complete, inspected PPE\n61\t- Verify insulated gloves are tested before each use\n62\t- Replace damaged PPE immediately\n63\t\n64\t**Workplace Hazard Awareness:**\n65\t- Conduct regular hazard inspections (daily walk-through)\n66\t- Verify HV warning signage is in place\n67\t- Check that barriers are used during HV work\n68\t- Ensure fire extinguishers are accessible and current\n69\t\n70\t**Accident Prevention:**\n71\t- Review and update SOPs regularly\n72\t- Investigate all incidents and near-misses\n73\t- Implement corrective actions from investigations\n74\t- Share lessons learned during daily briefings\n75\t\n76\t### 2.2 Waste Disposal Procedure Monitoring\n77\t- Verify proper storage, segregation, tagging, and signage of waste\n78\t- Monitor waste inventory logs\n79\t- Ensure timely pickup by licensed contractors\n80\t\n81\t### 2.3 Workshop Best Practice\n82\t**Do's:**\n83\t- Always de-energise HV before physical work\n84\t- Always verify zero-energy state with multimeter\n85\t- Always wear full PPE in BEV work bay\n86\t- Always use insulated tools for HV-adjacent work\n87\t- Always maintain two-person rule for HV work\n88\t\n89\t**Don'ts:**\n90\t- Never assume HV is disconnected — always verify\n91\t- Never wear metallic jewellery during HV work\n92\t- Never skip the capacitor discharge waiting period\n93\t- Never use damaged PPE or tools\n94\t- Never work alone on HV systems\n95\t\n96\t### 2.4 Emergency Response\n97\tThe supervisor ensures all personnel are trained in:\n98\t- **First aid:** Basic first aid, CPR, AED operation\n99\t- **Firefighting:** Class D fire extinguisher operation for lithium battery fires\n100\t- **Electric shock response:** Use of rescue hook, disconnection procedures, CPR/AED\n101\t- **Thermal runaway response:** Evacuation procedure, Class D extinguisher, BOMBA notification\n102\t\n103\t---\n104\t\n105\t## 3. SHE Finding Report\n106\t\n107\t### 3.1 Report Format and Contents\n108\t\n109\t| Section | Content |\n110\t|---------|---------|\n111\t| Date and time | When the finding was observed |\n112\t| Location | Specific area within the workshop |\n113\t| Finding description | What was observed (hazard, non-compliance, positive observation) |\n114\t| Photo evidence | Photos of the finding |\n115\t| Risk assessment | Severity and likelihood rating |\n116\t| Recommendation | Corrective action proposed |\n117\t| Responsible person | Who should action the recommendation |\n118\t| Target date | When the corrective action should be completed |\n119\t| Follow-up | Verification that action was taken |\n120\t\n121\t### 3.2 Risk Assessment Report\n122\tEach SHE finding should include a risk assessment:\n123\t- **Severity:** Minor / Moderate / Major / Critical\n124\t- **Likelihood:** Unlikely / Possible / Likely / Almost certain\n125\t- **Risk rating:** Low / Medium / High / Extreme\n126\t- **Photo documentation** of the finding\n127\t- **Date, time, and location** recorded\n128\t- **Recommendation** for corrective action\n129\t\n130\t### 3.3 Reporting to Superior\n131\t- Compile SHE findings into a periodic report (weekly or monthly)\n132\t- Highlight critical and high-risk findings requiring immediate attention\n133\t- Track corrective action completion status\n134\t- Submit report to superior per company procedure\n135\t\n136\t---\n137\t\n138\t## Rujukan\n139\t1. OSHA 1994 (Act 514)\n140\t2. Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127)\n141\t3. Company SHE policies and procedures\n142\t4. DOSH Guidelines for Occupational Safety and Health at Workplace\n143\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"3bdbbd33-dd42-4902-81ea-83d26969f291","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:18:12.640Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-04.md","content":"# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KP(4/4)\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** PUTIH / WHITE\n\n---\n\n## Work Activity 4: Monitor BEV Workshop Safety, Health & Environment\n\n## Objektif Pembelajaran\n1. Explain BEV workshop safety, health, and environment responsibilities\n2. Describe BEV-specific safety, health, and environment awareness requirements\n3. Explain the SHE finding report format and reporting process\n\n---\n\n## 1. BEV Safety, Health, and Environment (Keselamatan, Kesihatan dan Alam Sekitar)\n\n### 1.1 Employer and Employee Responsibilities\n\n**Employer responsibilities (under OSHA 1994):**\n- Provide a safe workplace with appropriate HV safety equipment\n- Ensure all BEV technicians are properly trained and certified\n- Maintain safety equipment in serviceable condition\n- Provide PPE at no cost to employees\n- Establish and enforce safety procedures\n- Report workplace accidents and dangerous occurrences\n\n**Employee responsibilities:**\n- Follow established safety procedures\n- Wear prescribed PPE at all times\n- Report hazards, incidents, and near-misses immediately\n- Not tamper with or disable safety equipment\n- Participate in safety training and briefings\n\n### 1.2 Workplace Safety and Health\nThe supervisor must monitor:\n- HV safety procedures are followed for every BEV job\n- PPE is worn correctly and consistently\n- Tools and equipment are in serviceable condition\n- Work permits are obtained for HV work\n- Two-person rule is observed for HV work\n- Emergency equipment is accessible and maintained\n\n### 1.3 Waste Management Monitoring\nThe supervisor ensures:\n- Scheduled waste is properly classified, contained, and labeled\n- Waste disposal records are maintained\n- Licensed waste contractors are used\n- DOE reporting requirements are met\n- Waste storage areas are compliant\n\n---\n\n## 2. BEV Safety, Health, and Environment Awareness\n\n### 2.1 Basics of Safety, Health, and Environment\n\n**Proper BEV PPE:**\n- Monitor that all technicians have complete, inspected PPE\n- Verify insulated gloves are tested before each use\n- Replace damaged PPE immediately\n\n**Workplace Hazard Awareness:**\n- Conduct regular hazard inspections (daily walk-through)\n- Verify HV warning signage is in place\n- Check that barriers are used during HV work\n- Ensure fire extinguishers are accessible and current\n\n**Accident Prevention:**\n- Review and update SOPs regularly\n- Investigate all incidents and near-misses\n- Implement corrective actions from investigations\n- Share lessons learned during daily briefings\n\n### 2.2 Waste Disposal Procedure Monitoring\n- Verify proper storage, segregation, tagging, and signage of waste\n- Monitor waste inventory logs\n- Ensure timely pickup by licensed contractors\n\n### 2.3 Workshop Best Practice\n**Do's:**\n- Always de-energise HV before physical work\n- Always verify zero-energy state with multimeter\n- Always wear full PPE in BEV work bay\n- Always use insulated tools for HV-adjacent work\n- Always maintain two-person rule for HV work\n\n**Don'ts:**\n- Never assume HV is disconnected — always verify\n- Never wear metallic jewellery during HV work\n- Never skip the capacitor discharge waiting period\n- Never use damaged PPE or tools\n- Never work alone on HV systems\n\n### 2.4 Emergency Response\nThe supervisor ensures all personnel are trained in:\n- **First aid:** Basic first aid, CPR, AED operation\n- **Firefighting:** Class D fire extinguisher operation for lithium battery fires\n- **Electric shock response:** Use of rescue hook, disconnection procedures, CPR/AED\n- **Thermal runaway response:** Evacuation procedure, Class D extinguisher, BOMBA notification\n\n---\n\n## 3. SHE Finding Report\n\n### 3.1 Report Format and Contents\n\n| Section | Content |\n|---------|---------|\n| Date and time | When the finding was observed |\n| Location | Specific area within the workshop |\n| Finding description | What was observed (hazard, non-compliance, positive observation) |\n| Photo evidence | Photos of the finding |\n| Risk assessment | Severity and likelihood rating |\n| Recommendation | Corrective action proposed |\n| Responsible person | Who should action the recommendation |\n| Target date | When the corrective action should be completed |\n| Follow-up | Verification that action was taken |\n\n### 3.2 Risk Assessment Report\nEach SHE finding should include a risk assessment:\n- **Severity:** Minor / Moderate / Major / Critical\n- **Likelihood:** Unlikely / Possible / Likely / Almost certain\n- **Risk rating:** Low / Medium / High / Extreme\n- **Photo documentation** of the finding\n- **Date, time, and location** recorded\n- **Recommendation** for corrective action\n\n### 3.3 Reporting to Superior\n- Compile SHE findings into a periodic report (weekly or monthly)\n- Highlight critical and high-risk findings requiring immediate attention\n- Track corrective action completion status\n- Submit report to superior per company procedure\n\n---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. OSHA 1994 (Act 514)\n2. Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127)\n3. Company SHE policies and procedures\n4. DOSH Guidelines for Occupational Safety and Health at Workplace\n","numLines":143,"startLine":1,"totalLines":143}}}
