{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01X4LK99VBxX2afZDURMgwcU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# KERTAS TUGASAN / ASSIGNMENT SHEET\n2\t\n3\t**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KT(3/4)\n4\t**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** MERAH JAMBU / PINK\n5\t\n6\t## Work Activity 3: Coordinate BEV Subordinate's Training | **Reference:** C05/KP(3/4)\n7\t\n8\t### S1 — Short Answer: a) What is TNI (Training Needs Identification)? b) What is TNA (Training Needs Analysis)? Explain how they differ.\n9\t### S2 — Short Answer: Create a simple skills matrix for 3 technicians and 4 BEV competencies. Identify which technician needs what training.\n10\t### S3 — MCQ: Which is NOT a source for BEV training? A. JPK-accredited centres B. Manufacturer training academies C. Social media influencers D. Industry associations\n11\t### S4 — T/F: a) A skills chart maps each technician against required competencies. b) Training notification should be given on the day of training. c) Post-training evaluation includes checking if the technician can now perform the skill.\n12\t### S5 — Short Answer: List FIVE (5) training logistics items that must be arranged when coordinating subordinate training.\n13\t\n14\t## Skema Jawapan\n15\t**S1:** a) TNI: identifying what training is needed based on skill gaps, business needs, new technology. b) TNA: analysing the gap between current and required competency levels, then prioritising and selecting appropriate training interventions. TNI identifies needs; TNA analyses and prioritises them.\n16\t**S2:** Example matrix with Y/N entries showing skill gaps; training recommended where N. (Accept any reasonable matrix.)\n17\t**S3:** C\n18\t**S4:** a) TRUE b) FALSE — minimum 2 weeks advance notice c) TRUE\n19\t**S5:** Training location, training provider, training notification (2 weeks), work coverage, materials and facilities.\n20\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"c0515efc-a20f-4cf0-a8e4-ff47ae383fde","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:18:21.669Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KT-03.md","content":"# KERTAS TUGASAN / ASSIGNMENT SHEET\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KT(3/4)\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** MERAH JAMBU / PINK\n\n## Work Activity 3: Coordinate BEV Subordinate's Training | **Reference:** C05/KP(3/4)\n\n### S1 — Short Answer: a) What is TNI (Training Needs Identification)? b) What is TNA (Training Needs Analysis)? Explain how they differ.\n### S2 — Short Answer: Create a simple skills matrix for 3 technicians and 4 BEV competencies. Identify which technician needs what training.\n### S3 — MCQ: Which is NOT a source for BEV training? A. JPK-accredited centres B. Manufacturer training academies C. Social media influencers D. Industry associations\n### S4 — T/F: a) A skills chart maps each technician against required competencies. b) Training notification should be given on the day of training. c) Post-training evaluation includes checking if the technician can now perform the skill.\n### S5 — Short Answer: List FIVE (5) training logistics items that must be arranged when coordinating subordinate training.\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** a) TNI: identifying what training is needed based on skill gaps, business needs, new technology. b) TNA: analysing the gap between current and required competency levels, then prioritising and selecting appropriate training interventions. TNI identifies needs; TNA analyses and prioritises them.\n**S2:** Example matrix with Y/N entries showing skill gaps; training recommended where N. (Accept any reasonable matrix.)\n**S3:** C\n**S4:** a) TRUE b) FALSE — minimum 2 weeks advance notice c) TRUE\n**S5:** Training location, training provider, training notification (2 weeks), work coverage, materials and facilities.\n","numLines":20,"startLine":1,"totalLines":20}}}
