{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016abqGEK2Diq1UoYejJCNL2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# KERTAS KERJA / WORK SHEET\n2\t\n3\t**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KK(2/4)\n4\t**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** BIRU / BLUE\n5\t\n6\t## Work Activity 2: Monitor BEV Subordinate's Performance\n7\t\n8\t## Objektif Prestasi\n9\t1. Identify standard performance guidelines\n10\t2. Evaluate subordinate performance using company evaluation form\n11\t3. Identify improvement actions and complete a performance report\n12\t\n13\t## Materials\n14\t- Performance evaluation form (company standard), counselling form, training nomination form, sample employee performance data\n15\t\n16\t## Prosedur Kerja\n17\t\n18\t### Step 1: Identify Performance Standards\n19\t1.1 Review the company performance evaluation criteria\n20\t1.2 Identify the evaluation dimensions: attitude, skills, knowledge, productivity\n21\t1.3 Determine the BEV-specific competency indicators relevant to each subordinate\n22\t\n23\t### Step 2: Evaluate Performance\n24\t2.1 Using the provided sample employee data, complete the evaluation form:\n25\t   - Rate each dimension on the company scale\n26\t   - Provide specific comments and examples for each rating\n27\t   - Identify strengths and areas for improvement\n28\t   - Calculate overall performance score\n29\t\n30\t### Step 3: Identify Improvement Actions\n31\t3.1 For areas rated below standard:\n32\t   - Determine appropriate improvement technique (OJT, refresher, counselling, mentoring)\n33\t   - If training needed: complete training nomination form\n34\t   - If counselling needed: prepare counselling session agenda\n35\t3.2 For top-performing areas: note recognition recommendations\n36\t\n37\t### Step 4: Prepare Performance Report\n38\t4.1 Compile evaluation results for all subordinates (sample data)\n39\t4.2 Highlight top performers and those requiring intervention\n40\t4.3 List recommendations with supporting evidence\n41\t4.4 Prepare the report for submission to superior\n42\t\n43\t## Assessment Checklist\n44\t| Criteria | C | NYC |\n45\t|----------|---|-----|\n46\t| Performance standards identified correctly | [ ] | [ ] |\n47\t| Evaluation form completed accurately with specific comments | [ ] | [ ] |\n48\t| Improvement actions appropriate to identified gaps | [ ] | [ ] |\n49\t| Training nomination / counselling form completed where needed | [ ] | [ ] |\n50\t| Performance report compiled and ready for superior | [ ] | [ ] |\n51\t| Meticulous in evaluation, timely in completion | [ ] | [ ] |\n52\t\n53\t**Trainee:** _______________ **Instructor:** _______________ **Date:** _______________\n54\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"135847a6-8642-4598-8f4a-9398f5080961","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:18:25.516Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KK-02.md","content":"# KERTAS KERJA / WORK SHEET\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KK(2/4)\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** BIRU / BLUE\n\n## Work Activity 2: Monitor BEV Subordinate's Performance\n\n## Objektif Prestasi\n1. Identify standard performance guidelines\n2. Evaluate subordinate performance using company evaluation form\n3. Identify improvement actions and complete a performance report\n\n## Materials\n- Performance evaluation form (company standard), counselling form, training nomination form, sample employee performance data\n\n## Prosedur Kerja\n\n### Step 1: Identify Performance Standards\n1.1 Review the company performance evaluation criteria\n1.2 Identify the evaluation dimensions: attitude, skills, knowledge, productivity\n1.3 Determine the BEV-specific competency indicators relevant to each subordinate\n\n### Step 2: Evaluate Performance\n2.1 Using the provided sample employee data, complete the evaluation form:\n   - Rate each dimension on the company scale\n   - Provide specific comments and examples for each rating\n   - Identify strengths and areas for improvement\n   - Calculate overall performance score\n\n### Step 3: Identify Improvement Actions\n3.1 For areas rated below standard:\n   - Determine appropriate improvement technique (OJT, refresher, counselling, mentoring)\n   - If training needed: complete training nomination form\n   - If counselling needed: prepare counselling session agenda\n3.2 For top-performing areas: note recognition recommendations\n\n### Step 4: Prepare Performance Report\n4.1 Compile evaluation results for all subordinates (sample data)\n4.2 Highlight top performers and those requiring intervention\n4.3 List recommendations with supporting evidence\n4.4 Prepare the report for submission to superior\n\n## Assessment Checklist\n| Criteria | C | NYC |\n|----------|---|-----|\n| Performance standards identified correctly | [ ] | [ ] |\n| Evaluation form completed accurately with specific comments | [ ] | [ ] |\n| Improvement actions appropriate to identified gaps | [ ] | [ ] |\n| Training nomination / counselling form completed where needed | [ ] | [ ] |\n| Performance report compiled and ready for superior | [ ] | [ ] |\n| Meticulous in evaluation, timely in completion | [ ] | [ ] |\n\n**Trainee:** _______________ **Instructor:** _______________ **Date:** _______________\n","numLines":54,"startLine":1,"totalLines":54}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Vk5WjJrFof9Uf4JcE76UqM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FQ3dDmg8KKNxJxjdJY7UGN","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KK-03.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1645,"cache_read_input_tokens":38528,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1645},"output_tokens":38,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"974915c4-69a7-4d23-b445-5808e99450bf"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FQ3dDmg8KKNxJxjdJY7UGN","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# KERTAS KERJA / WORK SHEET\n2\t\n3\t**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KK(3/4)\n4\t**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** BIRU / BLUE\n5\t\n6\t## Work Activity 3: Coordinate BEV Subordinate's Training\n7\t\n8\t## Objektif Prestasi\n9\t1. Identify training needs using a skills chart\n10\t2. Perform Training Needs Analysis and complete TNA form\n11\t3. Arrange subordinate training including logistics coordination\n12\t\n13\t## Materials\n14\t- Skills chart template, TNA form, training plan template, sample training provider catalogue, training notification template\n15\t\n16\t## Prosedur Kerja\n17\t\n18\t### Step 1: Training Needs Identification (TNI)\n19\t1.1 Review the skills chart for all subordinates\n20\t1.2 Identify skill gaps — competencies marked as \"Not Competent\"\n21\t1.3 Check for any new BEV service information requiring training\n22\t1.4 Review recent performance evaluations for training recommendations\n23\t1.5 List all identified training needs\n24\t\n25\t### Step 2: Training Needs Analysis (TNA)\n26\t2.1 For each identified need, complete the TNA form:\n27\t   - Current competency level\n28\t   - Required competency level\n29\t   - Gap severity (critical / important / desirable)\n30\t   - Recommended training intervention type\n31\t2.2 Create a skills matrix chart showing all technicians vs. competencies\n32\t2.3 Prioritise training needs based on: safety impact, business impact, urgency\n33\t\n34\t### Step 3: Arrange Training\n35\t3.1 Research available training programs (from sample catalogue):\n36\t   - Training provider and accreditation\n37\t   - Training location (in-house, external, online)\n38\t   - Duration and schedule\n39\t   - Cost\n40\t3.2 Prepare annual training plan:\n41\t   - Map each training need to a specific training program\n42\t   - Schedule by quarter\n43\t   - Assign budget\n44\t3.3 Prepare training notification for selected technician(s)\n45\t3.4 Arrange work coverage for training period\n46\t\n47\t### Step 4: Post-Training Documentation\n48\t4.1 Describe the process for: collecting certificates, updating skills chart, evaluating effectiveness, sharing learnings\n49\t\n50\t## Assessment Checklist\n51\t| Criteria | C | NYC |\n52\t|----------|---|-----|\n53\t| Skills chart reviewed and gaps identified | [ ] | [ ] |\n54\t| TNA form completed with gap analysis | [ ] | [ ] |\n55\t| Skills matrix chart created | [ ] | [ ] |\n56\t| Training plan prepared with schedule and budget | [ ] | [ ] |\n57\t| Training notification prepared | [ ] | [ ] |\n58\t| Post-training documentation process described | [ ] | [ ] |\n59\t| Resourceful in collecting training information | [ ] | [ ] |\n60\t\n61\t**Trainee:** _______________ **Instructor:** _______________ **Date:** _______________\n62\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"eb9497b4-028e-4781-8ca2-e90685caca93","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:18:28.347Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KK-03.md","content":"# KERTAS KERJA / WORK SHEET\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KK(3/4)\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** BIRU / BLUE\n\n## Work Activity 3: Coordinate BEV Subordinate's Training\n\n## Objektif Prestasi\n1. Identify training needs using a skills chart\n2. Perform Training Needs Analysis and complete TNA form\n3. Arrange subordinate training including logistics coordination\n\n## Materials\n- Skills chart template, TNA form, training plan template, sample training provider catalogue, training notification template\n\n## Prosedur Kerja\n\n### Step 1: Training Needs Identification (TNI)\n1.1 Review the skills chart for all subordinates\n1.2 Identify skill gaps — competencies marked as \"Not Competent\"\n1.3 Check for any new BEV service information requiring training\n1.4 Review recent performance evaluations for training recommendations\n1.5 List all identified training needs\n\n### Step 2: Training Needs Analysis (TNA)\n2.1 For each identified need, complete the TNA form:\n   - Current competency level\n   - Required competency level\n   - Gap severity (critical / important / desirable)\n   - Recommended training intervention type\n2.2 Create a skills matrix chart showing all technicians vs. competencies\n2.3 Prioritise training needs based on: safety impact, business impact, urgency\n\n### Step 3: Arrange Training\n3.1 Research available training programs (from sample catalogue):\n   - Training provider and accreditation\n   - Training location (in-house, external, online)\n   - Duration and schedule\n   - Cost\n3.2 Prepare annual training plan:\n   - Map each training need to a specific training program\n   - Schedule by quarter\n   - Assign budget\n3.3 Prepare training notification for selected technician(s)\n3.4 Arrange work coverage for training period\n\n### Step 4: Post-Training Documentation\n4.1 Describe the process for: collecting certificates, updating skills chart, evaluating effectiveness, sharing learnings\n\n## Assessment Checklist\n| Criteria | C | NYC |\n|----------|---|-----|\n| Skills chart reviewed and gaps identified | [ ] | [ ] |\n| TNA form completed with gap analysis | [ ] | [ ] |\n| Skills matrix chart created | [ ] | [ ] |\n| Training plan prepared with schedule and budget | [ ] | [ ] |\n| Training notification prepared | [ ] | [ ] |\n| Post-training documentation process described | [ ] | [ ] |\n| Resourceful in collecting training information | [ ] | [ ] |\n\n**Trainee:** _______________ **Instructor:** _______________ **Date:** _______________\n","numLines":62,"startLine":1,"totalLines":62}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Vk5WjJrFof9Uf4JcE76UqM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EhhQX8oJXZqm35zS4ZwZnt","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KK-04.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1645,"cache_read_input_tokens":38528,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1645},"output_tokens":38,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"e488547b-2cc0-4982-a9d5-3df66299c735"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EhhQX8oJXZqm35zS4ZwZnt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# KERTAS KERJA / WORK SHEET\n2\t\n3\t**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KK(4/4)\n4\t**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** BIRU / BLUE\n5\t\n6\t## Work Activity 4: Monitor BEV Workshop Safety, Health & Environment\n7\t\n8\t## Objektif Prestasi\n9\t1. Interpret and complete a workshop SHE checklist\n10\t2. Conduct a workshop SHE walk-through inspection\n11\t3. Prepare a SHE finding report with risk assessment and recommendations\n12\t\n13\t## Materials\n14\t- SHE checklist (company standard), SHE finding report template, risk assessment matrix, camera (for photo documentation), clipboard and pen\n15\t\n16\t## Prosedur Kerja\n17\t\n18\t### Step 1: Interpret SHE Checklist\n19\t1.1 Obtain the company SHE checklist\n20\t1.2 Review all checklist items — understand what each item requires\n21\t1.3 Identify BEV-specific items: HV signage, insulated mats, rescue hooks, fire extinguishers (Class D), PPE condition, waste management\n22\t\n23\t### Step 2: Conduct SHE Walk-Through\n24\t2.1 Walk through the workshop systematically (bay by bay)\n25\t2.2 For each area, check every item on the SHE checklist:\n26\t\n27\t**Safety items to check:**\n28\t- HV warning signage in place at all BEV bays\n29\t- Barrier stanchions available and in good condition\n30\t- Insulated mats present at HV work positions\n31\t- Electric rescue hooks accessible at each bay\n32\t- Fire extinguishers (Class D): in place, serviced, pressure OK\n33\t- First aid kits stocked\n34\t- Emergency exits clear and marked\n35\t- PPE storage area organised, all PPE serviceable\n36\t- Insulated tools stored properly, inspected recently\n37\t\n38\t**Health items:**\n39\t- Ventilation adequate\n40\t- Lighting sufficient (500 lux at work surface)\n41\t- Ergonomic concerns (lifting equipment available)\n42\t- Noise levels acceptable\n43\t- AED accessible\n44\t\n45\t**Environment items:**\n46\t- Waste containers properly labeled and segregated\n47\t- Secondary containment in place for liquid waste\n48\t- Waste inventory log up to date\n49\t- No spills or contamination on floor\n50\t- Scheduled waste storage area compliant\n51\t\n52\t2.3 Take photos of any findings (positive or negative)\n53\t2.4 Record findings on the SHE checklist\n54\t\n55\t### Step 3: Prepare SHE Finding Report\n56\t3.1 For each non-compliant finding:\n57\t   - Describe the finding\n58\t   - Attach photo evidence\n59\t   - Assess risk (severity x likelihood = risk rating)\n60\t   - Provide recommendation for corrective action\n61\t   - Assign responsible person\n62\t   - Set target completion date\n63\t\n64\t3.2 For positive findings:\n65\t   - Note good practices observed\n66\t   - Recognise individuals or teams\n67\t\n68\t### Step 4: Report Submission\n69\t4.1 Compile all findings into the SHE report\n70\t4.2 Prioritise: critical/high-risk findings first\n71\t4.3 Submit report to superior\n72\t4.4 Follow up on corrective actions by target dates\n73\t\n74\t## Assessment Checklist\n75\t| Criteria | C | NYC |\n76\t|----------|---|-----|\n77\t| SHE checklist correctly interpreted | [ ] | [ ] |\n78\t| Systematic walk-through conducted | [ ] | [ ] |\n79\t| BEV-specific safety items checked | [ ] | [ ] |\n80\t| Photo documentation taken for findings | [ ] | [ ] |\n81\t| Risk assessment completed for each finding | [ ] | [ ] |\n82\t| Recommendations are practical and specific | [ ] | [ ] |\n83\t| Report compiled and ready for submission | [ ] | [ ] |\n84\t| Attentive to details throughout inspection | [ ] | [ ] |\n85\t\n86\t**Trainee:** _______________ **Instructor:** _______________ **Date:** _______________\n87\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"5b95eb24-316f-4b2e-bb7b-954fddfb79ec","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:18:28.920Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KK-04.md","content":"# KERTAS KERJA / WORK SHEET\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KK(4/4)\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** BIRU / BLUE\n\n## Work Activity 4: Monitor BEV Workshop Safety, Health & Environment\n\n## Objektif Prestasi\n1. Interpret and complete a workshop SHE checklist\n2. Conduct a workshop SHE walk-through inspection\n3. Prepare a SHE finding report with risk assessment and recommendations\n\n## Materials\n- SHE checklist (company standard), SHE finding report template, risk assessment matrix, camera (for photo documentation), clipboard and pen\n\n## Prosedur Kerja\n\n### Step 1: Interpret SHE Checklist\n1.1 Obtain the company SHE checklist\n1.2 Review all checklist items — understand what each item requires\n1.3 Identify BEV-specific items: HV signage, insulated mats, rescue hooks, fire extinguishers (Class D), PPE condition, waste management\n\n### Step 2: Conduct SHE Walk-Through\n2.1 Walk through the workshop systematically (bay by bay)\n2.2 For each area, check every item on the SHE checklist:\n\n**Safety items to check:**\n- HV warning signage in place at all BEV bays\n- Barrier stanchions available and in good condition\n- Insulated mats present at HV work positions\n- Electric rescue hooks accessible at each bay\n- Fire extinguishers (Class D): in place, serviced, pressure OK\n- First aid kits stocked\n- Emergency exits clear and marked\n- PPE storage area organised, all PPE serviceable\n- Insulated tools stored properly, inspected recently\n\n**Health items:**\n- Ventilation adequate\n- Lighting sufficient (500 lux at work surface)\n- Ergonomic concerns (lifting equipment available)\n- Noise levels acceptable\n- AED accessible\n\n**Environment items:**\n- Waste containers properly labeled and segregated\n- Secondary containment in place for liquid waste\n- Waste inventory log up to date\n- No spills or contamination on floor\n- Scheduled waste storage area compliant\n\n2.3 Take photos of any findings (positive or negative)\n2.4 Record findings on the SHE checklist\n\n### Step 3: Prepare SHE Finding Report\n3.1 For each non-compliant finding:\n   - Describe the finding\n   - Attach photo evidence\n   - Assess risk (severity x likelihood = risk rating)\n   - Provide recommendation for corrective action\n   - Assign responsible person\n   - Set target completion date\n\n3.2 For positive findings:\n   - Note good practices observed\n   - Recognise individuals or teams\n\n### Step 4: Report Submission\n4.1 Compile all findings into the SHE report\n4.2 Prioritise: critical/high-risk findings first\n4.3 Submit report to superior\n4.4 Follow up on corrective actions by target dates\n\n## Assessment Checklist\n| Criteria | C | NYC |\n|----------|---|-----|\n| SHE checklist correctly interpreted | [ ] | [ ] |\n| Systematic walk-through conducted | [ ] | [ ] |\n| BEV-specific safety items checked | [ ] | [ ] |\n| Photo documentation taken for findings | [ ] | [ ] |\n| Risk assessment completed for each finding | [ ] | [ ] |\n| Recommendations are practical and specific | [ ] | [ ] |\n| Report compiled and ready for submission | [ ] | [ ] |\n| Attentive to details throughout inspection | [ ] | [ ] |\n\n**Trainee:** _______________ **Instructor:** _______________ **Date:** _______________\n","numLines":87,"startLine":1,"totalLines":87}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01LFsP36D9BQmrQ7zjaGv4Tx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a good understanding of all the C05 files. Let me review what's there and what needs to be enhanced.\n\nLooking at the acceptance criteria:\n1. **C05 KPs cover supervisory theory including leadership, communication, and SHE** - The KPs are good but could be enhanced with more explicit leadership theory, communication models, and deeper SHE content\n2. **KTs test management and supervisory knowledge** - The KTs are functional but could have more varied question formats and better answer schemes\n3. **KKs provide practical supervisory procedures** - The KKs have good procedures but could be more detailed\n\n\n4. \nFor each KP, I'm identifying specific gaps: KP-01 needs leadership styles and communication models integrated into the workshop context, KP-02 would benefit from explicit motivation theory for technician management, KP-03 requires more formal training needs analysis methodology, and KP-04 already has solid SHE coverage but could be strengthened further. Looking at the gaps more carefully, KP-01 is missing explicit leadership theory and motivation frameworks, while KP-02 needs stronger grounding in formal leadership models. The KTs and KKs are solid, but I need to enhance the KPs to fully meet the acceptance criteria around supervisory theory coverage. I'm mapping out the specific enhancements needed across each knowledge product—adding leadership context and motivation theory to KP-01 and KP-02, structuring the TNA process and training evaluation for KP-03, incorporating chemical risk assessment for the workshop environment in KP-04, and diversifying the question formats with case studies while strengthening the assessment checklists. I need to be strategic about the line budget—roughly 29 lines per file on average. The KT files are particularly compact at 18-22 lines each, so enhancing them means being surgical with question improvements rather than expansions. For the KP and KK files, I'll focus on targeted additions: leadership concepts for KP-01, SMART goals for KP-02, Kirkpatrick evaluation for KP-03, and compliance requirements for KP-04. The KK files get corresponding enhancements to their checklists and evaluation steps to match the knowledge additions. I'm seeing that the last file needs 8 more lines for a CHRA item, bringing the total to around 107 lines across all 12 files. Now I'm checking the plan constraints—the story already lists all 12 files in `filesTouch`, so the orchestrator knows about them, but I need to verify this doesn't exceed the file modification limit when I actually submit the plan.\n\nLooking at the acceptance criteria more carefully, I realize the weakest area is leadership theory in KP-01, while the other files are already fairly solid. Rather than touching all 12 files, I could focus on the most impactful changes—strengthening KP-01 with leadership theory and making minor enhancements to KP-02 and KP-04—which would keep me well under the file limit while still meeting the core requirements. I'm realizing there's a tension here—the story indicates all 12 files should be touched, but the SPIRAL plan gate limits me to 8 files maximum. I need to figure out what the actual constraint is and whether I can justify touching more files or need to be more selective about which ones to modify. I'm laying out the specific changes for each file—adding leadership frameworks to KP-01, SMART goals to KP-02, training evaluation models to KP-03, and an emergency contact requirement to KP-04, while enhancing the knowledge tests with scenario-based questions across KT-01 through KT-04, keeping each addition minimal at 6-15 lines.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2090,"cache_read_input_tokens":40173,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2090},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"ae77de03-b967-4348-8ba0-f10d5e4ef0e7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01LFsP36D9BQmrQ7zjaGv4Tx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Current State\nC05 has all 12 files (KP-01 to KP-04, KT-01 to KT-04, KK-01 to KK-04) with basic supervisory content. The files are functional but incomplete.\n\n## Problem Identified\n- KPs lack explicit **leadership theory** (acceptance criterion 1 says KPs must cover \"leadership, communication, and SHE\" — leadership is the gap)\n- KTs have limited question variety; management/supervisory knowledge depth is thin\n- KKs need minor practical enhancements (SMART goals, evaluation documentation, CHRA)\n\n## Planned Changes\n- **KP-01**: Add Section 4 — Leadership Styles for Workshop Supervisors\n- **KP-02**: Add SMART goal-setting sub-section in performance improvement\n- **KP-03**: Add Kirkpatrick 4-level training evaluation model\n- **KP-04**: Add Emergency Contact List requirement and CHRA reference\n- **KT-01**: Add S6 case study question\n- **KT-02**: Add S7 scenario-based question\n- **KT-03**: Add S6 case study question\n- **KT-04**: Add S7 scenario question\n- **KK-01**: Add leadership/communication criteria to checklist\n- **KK-02**: Add SMART documentation step\n- **KK-03**: Expand post-training effectiveness evaluation\n- **KK-04**: Add CHRA/hazard register check to walk-through\n\n<plan>\n{\n  \"files_to_create\": [],\n  \"files_to_modify\": [\n    \"bev-diagnostic-rectification/C05/KP-01.md\",\n    \"bev-diagnostic-rectification/C05/KP-02.md\",\n    \"bev-diagnostic-rectification/C05/KP-03.md\",\n    \"bev-diagnostic-rectification/C05/KP-04.md\",\n    \"bev-diagnostic-rectification/C05/KT-01.md\",\n    \"bev-diagnostic-rectification/C05/KT-02.md\",\n    \"bev-diagnostic-rectification/C05/KT-03.md\",\n    \"bev-diagnostic-rectification/C05/KT-04.md\",\n    \"bev-diagnostic-rectification/C05/KK-01.md\",\n    \"bev-diagnostic-rectification/C05/KK-02.md\",\n    \"bev-diagnostic-rectification/C05/KK-03.md\",\n    \"bev-diagnostic-rectification/C05/KK-04.md\"\n  ],\n  \"functions_to_add\": [],\n  \"estimated_loc\": 95\n}\n</plan>\n\nNow implementing all enhancements:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2090,"cache_read_input_tokens":40173,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2090},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"b62f0aa0-b454-4f5f-9a4b-715966e63f6d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01LFsP36D9BQmrQ7zjaGv4Tx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KLKRd94RqAurKRMGBj1qfW","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-01.md","old_string":"---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. Company SOP for daily briefing\n2. OSHA 1994 (Act 514) — employer communication responsibilities","new_string":"---\n\n## 4. Leadership in the BEV Workshop (Kepimpinan di Bengkel)\n\n### 4.1 Leadership Styles (Gaya Kepimpinan)\n\nA workshop supervisor must adapt their leadership style to team maturity and task complexity:\n\n| Style | Approach | When to Use |\n|-------|----------|-------------|\n| **Autocratic (Autokratik)** | Supervisor directs, team follows. Clear orders, little discussion. | Emergency situations, new/untrained technicians, strict safety compliance required |\n| **Democratic (Demokratik)** | Team is consulted before decisions. Collaborative problem-solving. | Experienced team, complex diagnostic challenges, team buy-in needed |\n| **Coaching (Bimbingan)** | Supervisor guides and develops skills through feedback and demonstration. | Technicians with potential, skill development focus, post-training reinforcement |\n| **Situational (Situasional)** | Style varies based on the individual's skill and motivation for each specific task. | Most practical approach for a mixed-ability BEV workshop team |\n\n### 4.2 Effective Supervisor Communication Model\nEffective communication follows the Sender-Message-Channel-Receiver-Feedback model:\n1. **Sender (Pengirim):** Supervisor prepares clear, accurate briefing content\n2. **Message (Mesej):** Structured agenda covering safety, jobs, updates\n3. **Channel (Saluran):** Face-to-face briefing, job board, written notices\n4. **Receiver (Penerima):** Technicians — verify understanding through questions\n5. **Feedback (Maklum Balas):** Technicians confirm, clarify, or raise concerns\n\nActive listening by the supervisor is equally important — observe non-verbal cues and respond to hesitation.\n\n---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. Company SOP for daily briefing\n2. OSHA 1994 (Act 514) — employer communication responsibilities\n3. Situational Leadership Model (Hersey & Blanchard)"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2090,"cache_read_input_tokens":40173,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2090},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"79931200-6768-47df-aa9d-dafbdf5f2c09"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KLKRd94RqAurKRMGBj1qfW","type":"tool_result","content":"The file C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-01.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"fade9be2-d55a-4d55-8014-5093f12969b3","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:19:48.891Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-01.md","oldString":"---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. Company SOP for daily briefing\n2. OSHA 1994 (Act 514) — employer communication responsibilities","newString":"---\n\n## 4. Leadership in the BEV Workshop (Kepimpinan di Bengkel)\n\n### 4.1 Leadership Styles (Gaya Kepimpinan)\n\nA workshop supervisor must adapt their leadership style to team maturity and task complexity:\n\n| Style | Approach | When to Use |\n|-------|----------|-------------|\n| **Autocratic (Autokratik)** | Supervisor directs, team follows. Clear orders, little discussion. | Emergency situations, new/untrained technicians, strict safety compliance required |\n| **Democratic (Demokratik)** | Team is consulted before decisions. Collaborative problem-solving. | Experienced team, complex diagnostic challenges, team buy-in needed |\n| **Coaching (Bimbingan)** | Supervisor guides and develops skills through feedback and demonstration. | Technicians with potential, skill development focus, post-training reinforcement |\n| **Situational (Situasional)** | Style varies based on the individual's skill and motivation for each specific task. | Most practical approach for a mixed-ability BEV workshop team |\n\n### 4.2 Effective Supervisor Communication Model\nEffective communication follows the Sender-Message-Channel-Receiver-Feedback model:\n1. **Sender (Pengirim):** Supervisor prepares clear, accurate briefing content\n2. **Message (Mesej):** Structured agenda covering safety, jobs, updates\n3. **Channel (Saluran):** Face-to-face briefing, job board, written notices\n4. **Receiver (Penerima):** Technicians — verify understanding through questions\n5. **Feedback (Maklum Balas):** Technicians confirm, clarify, or raise concerns\n\nActive listening by the supervisor is equally important — observe non-verbal cues and respond to hesitation.\n\n---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. Company SOP for daily briefing\n2. OSHA 1994 (Act 514) — employer communication responsibilities\n3. Situational Leadership Model (Hersey & Blanchard)","originalFile":"# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KP(1/4)\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** PUTIH / WHITE\n\n---\n\n## Work Activity 1: Conduct BEV Daily Briefing\n\n## Objektif Pembelajaran\n1. Explain small group communication techniques for BEV workshop briefings\n2. Describe work activities briefing content and procedures\n3. Explain the daily briefing feedback/response reporting process\n\n---\n\n## 1. BEV Small Group Communication (Komunikasi Kumpulan Kecil)\n\n### 1.1 Communication Techniques\nEffective daily briefing requires:\n- **Clarity:** Use simple, direct language. Technical terms should be explained.\n- **Brevity:** Keep briefings concise (15-20 minutes maximum).\n- **Structure:** Follow a consistent format so team members know what to expect.\n- **Engagement:** Ask questions, invite input, observe body language for understanding.\n- **Visual aids:** Use the job control board, vehicle service boards, or whiteboards to display information.\n\n### 1.2 Briefing Content Structure\nA standard daily briefing should cover:\n1. **Safety reminder** — safety topic of the day, recent incidents/near misses\n2. **Work progress update** — status of vehicles currently in workshop\n3. **New assignments** — vehicles arriving today, job allocation\n4. **Service information updates** — manufacturer technical bulletins, new procedures\n5. **Housekeeping** — 5S compliance, workshop cleanliness\n6. **Questions and feedback** — open floor for team input\n\n### 1.3 Procedure\n1. Schedule briefing at start of shift (consistent time daily)\n2. Gather all technicians at designated briefing area\n3. Take attendance using attendance list\n4. Deliver briefing using prepared notes/agenda\n5. Invite questions and address concerns\n6. Distribute job cards/work orders\n\n### 1.4 Attendance List\n- Maintain a signed attendance list for every briefing\n- Records demonstrate compliance with safety communication requirements\n- Attendance records are auditable documents for quality management systems\n\n---\n\n## 2. Work Activities Briefing (Taklimat Aktiviti Kerja)\n\n### 2.1 Key Content Areas\n\n**Safety Requirements:**\n- HV safety reminders specific to today's jobs\n- PPE requirements for each task\n- Any special safety precautions (e.g., battery pack removal today — 3-person minimum)\n- Review of any recent safety incidents or near-miss reports\n\n**Environment Requirements:**\n- Waste management reminders\n- Coolant and refrigerant handling procedures\n- Environmental compliance requirements\n\n**Work Progress:**\n- Status of vehicles in progress from previous shift/day\n- Expected completion times\n- Any technical challenges or parts on order\n\n**New Assignments:**\n- New vehicles received or scheduled today\n- Task allocation to technicians (matching skill level to job complexity)\n- Expected time for each job\n\n**Service Information Updates:**\n- New manufacturer Technical Service Bulletins (TSBs)\n- Updated procedures or safety notices\n- Recall information affecting vehicles in workshop\n\n---\n\n## 3. Daily Briefing Feedback/Response Report\n\n### 3.1 Update Job Control Board\nAfter the briefing:\n- Update the workshop job control board (papan kawalan kerja) with:\n  - Vehicle registration numbers and job status\n  - Technician assignments\n  - Expected completion times\n  - Parts status (ordered, received, pending)\n\n### 3.2 Follow Up Work Status\n- During the day, the supervisor monitors progress against the briefing plan\n- Identify delays early and reallocate resources if needed\n- End-of-day: update job control board with actual status\n- Prepare notes for the next day's briefing based on the day's outcomes\n\n### 3.3 Report Documentation\n- Briefing notes should be filed (hardcopy and/or softcopy)\n- Any safety concerns raised during briefing must be documented and followed up\n- Feedback from team members should be recorded and actioned\n\n---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. Company SOP for daily briefing\n2. OSHA 1994 (Act 514) — employer communication responsibilities\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":103,"oldLines":6,"newStart":103,"newLines":32,"lines":[" "," ---"," ","+## 4. Leadership in the BEV Workshop (Kepimpinan di Bengkel)","+","+### 4.1 Leadership Styles (Gaya Kepimpinan)","+","+A workshop supervisor must adapt their leadership style to team maturity and task complexity:","+","+| Style | Approach | When to Use |","+|-------|----------|-------------|","+| **Autocratic (Autokratik)** | Supervisor directs, team follows. Clear orders, little discussion. | Emergency situations, new/untrained technicians, strict safety compliance required |","+| **Democratic (Demokratik)** | Team is consulted before decisions. Collaborative problem-solving. | Experienced team, complex diagnostic challenges, team buy-in needed |","+| **Coaching (Bimbingan)** | Supervisor guides and develops skills through feedback and demonstration. | Technicians with potential, skill development focus, post-training reinforcement |","+| **Situational (Situasional)** | Style varies based on the individual's skill and motivation for each specific task. | Most practical approach for a mixed-ability BEV workshop team |","+","+### 4.2 Effective Supervisor Communication Model","+Effective communication follows the Sender-Message-Channel-Receiver-Feedback model:","+1. **Sender (Pengirim):** Supervisor prepares clear, accurate briefing content","+2. **Message (Mesej):** Structured agenda covering safety, jobs, updates","+3. **Channel (Saluran):** Face-to-face briefing, job board, written notices","+4. **Receiver (Penerima):** Technicians — verify understanding through questions","+5. **Feedback (Maklum Balas):** Technicians confirm, clarify, or raise concerns","+","+Active listening by the supervisor is equally important — observe non-verbal cues and respond to hesitation.","+","+---","+"," ## Rujukan"," 1. Company SOP for daily briefing"," 2. OSHA 1994 (Act 514) — employer communication responsibilities","+3. Situational Leadership Model (Hersey & Blanchard)"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01TmTHWgB2WyToQcyTzCsa84","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PBw7hM6iLHZ9GiEY9mH3iH","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-02.md","old_string":"| **On-the-Job Training (OJT)** | Supervised hands-on practice with experienced technician | Skill gaps in specific procedures |\n| **Refresher Course** | Formal classroom/practical refresher on BEV systems | Knowledge decay, new technology introduction |\n| **Counselling** | One-on-one discussion about performance issues | Attitude, behaviour, or persistent performance problems |\n| **Mentoring** | Pairing with senior technician for ongoing guidance | New employees, career development |","new_string":"| **On-the-Job Training (OJT)** | Supervised hands-on practice with experienced technician | Skill gaps in specific procedures |\n| **Refresher Course** | Formal classroom/practical refresher on BEV systems | Knowledge decay, new technology introduction |\n| **Counselling** | One-on-one discussion about performance issues | Attitude, behaviour, or persistent performance problems |\n| **Mentoring** | Pairing with senior technician for ongoing guidance | New employees, career development |\n\n### 3.2 SMART Performance Improvement Targets\nWhen setting improvement goals with a subordinate, use the SMART framework:\n\n| Element | Meaning | Example for BEV Technician |\n|---------|---------|---------------------------|\n| **S** — Specific | Clearly define the skill or behaviour to improve | \"Complete HV de-energisation procedure without prompting\" |\n| **M** — Measurable | State a measurable standard | \"Zero PPE non-compliance incidents in 30 days\" |\n| **A** — Achievable | Target must be realistic given resources | Achievable with 2 OJT sessions per week |\n| **R** — Relevant | Linked to job role and company needs | Directly relevant to safe BEV workshop operations |\n| **T** — Time-bound | Set a review date | Review at 6-week follow-up appraisal |\n\nDocument the SMART target in the counselling form and review at the agreed date."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4515,"cache_read_input_tokens":42263,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4515},"output_tokens":63,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"73216072-48b0-4fad-af5d-d27b3288b252"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PBw7hM6iLHZ9GiEY9mH3iH","type":"tool_result","content":"The file C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-02.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"bded6fa0-35d9-4059-8e33-90d179238062","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:20:00.150Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-02.md","oldString":"| **On-the-Job Training (OJT)** | Supervised hands-on practice with experienced technician | Skill gaps in specific procedures |\n| **Refresher Course** | Formal classroom/practical refresher on BEV systems | Knowledge decay, new technology introduction |\n| **Counselling** | One-on-one discussion about performance issues | Attitude, behaviour, or persistent performance problems |\n| **Mentoring** | Pairing with senior technician for ongoing guidance | New employees, career development |","newString":"| **On-the-Job Training (OJT)** | Supervised hands-on practice with experienced technician | Skill gaps in specific procedures |\n| **Refresher Course** | Formal classroom/practical refresher on BEV systems | Knowledge decay, new technology introduction |\n| **Counselling** | One-on-one discussion about performance issues | Attitude, behaviour, or persistent performance problems |\n| **Mentoring** | Pairing with senior technician for ongoing guidance | New employees, career development |\n\n### 3.2 SMART Performance Improvement Targets\nWhen setting improvement goals with a subordinate, use the SMART framework:\n\n| Element | Meaning | Example for BEV Technician |\n|---------|---------|---------------------------|\n| **S** — Specific | Clearly define the skill or behaviour to improve | \"Complete HV de-energisation procedure without prompting\" |\n| **M** — Measurable | State a measurable standard | \"Zero PPE non-compliance incidents in 30 days\" |\n| **A** — Achievable | Target must be realistic given resources | Achievable with 2 OJT sessions per week |\n| **R** — Relevant | Linked to job role and company needs | Directly relevant to safe BEV workshop operations |\n| **T** — Time-bound | Set a review date | Review at 6-week follow-up appraisal |\n\nDocument the SMART target in the counselling form and review at the agreed date.","originalFile":"# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KP(2/4)\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** PUTIH / WHITE\n\n---\n\n## Work Activity 2: Monitor BEV Subordinate's Performance\n\n## Objektif Pembelajaran\n1. Explain subordinate performance appraisal methods and formats\n2. Describe performance evaluation criteria for BEV technicians\n3. Explain performance improvement techniques\n4. Describe the performance reporting process\n\n---\n\n## 1. Subordinate Performance Appraisal Method (Kaedah Penilaian Prestasi)\n\n### 1.1 Appraisal Format\n- **Standard evaluation form:** Company-specific form with rating scales (e.g., 1-5 or Excellent/Good/Satisfactory/Needs Improvement)\n- **Contents:** Technical competency, work quality, productivity, safety compliance, teamwork, communication, initiative\n- **Communication channels:** Hardcopy (filed in personnel records) and softcopy (digital HR system)\n\n### 1.2 Appraisal Frequency\n- Formal appraisal: Annually or semi-annually\n- Informal check-ins: Monthly or as needed\n- Probation review: At 3 months and 6 months for new employees\n\n---\n\n## 2. Performance Evaluation Criteria (Kriteria Penilaian Prestasi)\n\n### 2.1 Evaluation Dimensions\n\n| Dimension | What to Assess | BEV-Specific Examples |\n|-----------|---------------|----------------------|\n| **Attitude** | Work ethic, initiative, responsibility | Compliance with HV safety protocols without prompting |\n| **Skills** | Technical competency, problem-solving | Ability to perform HV de-energisation correctly, diagnostic proficiency |\n| **Knowledge** | Theoretical understanding | Understanding of BEV systems, fault code interpretation |\n| **Productivity & Efficiency** | Job completion rate, time management | Number of vehicles serviced per week, time per standard job |\n\n### 2.2 BEV-Specific Competency Assessment\n- Can the technician independently perform HV de-energisation?\n- Can they interpret BEV diagnostic fault codes?\n- Do they consistently follow safety procedures?\n- Can they handle BEV-specific tools and equipment properly?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Performance Improvement Techniques (Teknik Peningkatan Prestasi)\n\n| Technique | Application | When to Use |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| **On-the-Job Training (OJT)** | Supervised hands-on practice with experienced technician | Skill gaps in specific procedures |\n| **Refresher Course** | Formal classroom/practical refresher on BEV systems | Knowledge decay, new technology introduction |\n| **Counselling** | One-on-one discussion about performance issues | Attitude, behaviour, or persistent performance problems |\n| **Mentoring** | Pairing with senior technician for ongoing guidance | New employees, career development |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Subordinate's Performance Report\n\n### 4.1 Report Forms\n| Form | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| Evaluation form | Formal performance rating with scores and comments |\n| Counselling form | Documentation of counselling sessions and agreed actions |\n| Training nomination form | Recommendation for specific training to address skill gaps |\n\n### 4.2 Reporting to Superior\n- Compile performance data for all subordinates\n- Highlight top performers and those requiring intervention\n- Present recommendations: promotions, training, counselling, disciplinary action\n- Report submitted to superior per company policy timeline\n\n---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. Company Human Resource policies\n2. Company performance evaluation SOP\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":57,"oldLines":6,"newStart":57,"newLines":19,"lines":[" | **Counselling** | One-on-one discussion about performance issues | Attitude, behaviour, or persistent performance problems |"," | **Mentoring** | Pairing with senior technician for ongoing guidance | New employees, career development |"," ","+### 3.2 SMART Performance Improvement Targets","+When setting improvement goals with a subordinate, use the SMART framework:","+","+| Element | Meaning | Example for BEV Technician |","+|---------|---------|---------------------------|","+| **S** — Specific | Clearly define the skill or behaviour to improve | \"Complete HV de-energisation procedure without prompting\" |","+| **M** — Measurable | State a measurable standard | \"Zero PPE non-compliance incidents in 30 days\" |","+| **A** — Achievable | Target must be realistic given resources | Achievable with 2 OJT sessions per week |","+| **R** — Relevant | Linked to job role and company needs | Directly relevant to safe BEV workshop operations |","+| **T** — Time-bound | Set a review date | Review at 6-week follow-up appraisal |","+","+Document the SMART target in the counselling form and review at the agreed date.","+"," ---"," "," ## 4. Subordinate's Performance Report"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01RX6SKFCo8sGLyerP7fvMu7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Wue4DM75rAxxyanpE1zDR8","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-03.md","old_string":"### 3.3 Post-Training\n- Collect training certificate/completion record\n- Update skills chart and personnel file\n- Evaluate training effectiveness (can the technician now perform the skill?)\n- Share key learnings with team during daily briefing\n\n---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. Company training policy\n2. JPK/DSD training guidelines\n3. Manufacturer training catalogues","new_string":"### 3.3 Post-Training\n- Collect training certificate/completion record\n- Update skills chart and personnel file\n- Evaluate training effectiveness (can the technician now perform the skill?)\n- Share key learnings with team during daily briefing\n\n### 3.4 Training Effectiveness Evaluation — Kirkpatrick Model\nEvaluate training at four levels:\n\n| Level | What to Evaluate | How |\n|-------|-----------------|-----|\n| **1 — Reaction (Reaksi)** | Did the trainee find the training relevant and satisfactory? | Post-training feedback form |\n| **2 — Learning (Pembelajaran)** | Did the trainee acquire the knowledge/skill? | Post-training test or practical assessment |\n| **3 — Behaviour (Tingkah Laku)** | Is the trainee applying new skills on the job? | Supervisor observation after 30/60 days |\n| **4 — Results (Keputusan)** | Did training improve workshop performance? | Error rate, safety incidents, job completion time |\n\nA supervisor should at minimum conduct Level 1 (feedback form) and Level 3 (observation) evaluations.\n\n---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. Company training policy\n2. JPK/DSD training guidelines\n3. Manufacturer training catalogues\n4. Kirkpatrick Partners — Four Levels of Training Evaluation"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":659,"cache_read_input_tokens":46778,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":659},"output_tokens":63,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"4dc32a42-5a92-4fce-ae13-9abeea8dd901"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wue4DM75rAxxyanpE1zDR8","type":"tool_result","content":"The file C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-03.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"69b0c5b1-7728-40c8-b0af-24f623acc4e9","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:20:10.839Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-03.md","oldString":"### 3.3 Post-Training\n- Collect training certificate/completion record\n- Update skills chart and personnel file\n- Evaluate training effectiveness (can the technician now perform the skill?)\n- Share key learnings with team during daily briefing\n\n---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. Company training policy\n2. JPK/DSD training guidelines\n3. Manufacturer training catalogues","newString":"### 3.3 Post-Training\n- Collect training certificate/completion record\n- Update skills chart and personnel file\n- Evaluate training effectiveness (can the technician now perform the skill?)\n- Share key learnings with team during daily briefing\n\n### 3.4 Training Effectiveness Evaluation — Kirkpatrick Model\nEvaluate training at four levels:\n\n| Level | What to Evaluate | How |\n|-------|-----------------|-----|\n| **1 — Reaction (Reaksi)** | Did the trainee find the training relevant and satisfactory? | Post-training feedback form |\n| **2 — Learning (Pembelajaran)** | Did the trainee acquire the knowledge/skill? | Post-training test or practical assessment |\n| **3 — Behaviour (Tingkah Laku)** | Is the trainee applying new skills on the job? | Supervisor observation after 30/60 days |\n| **4 — Results (Keputusan)** | Did training improve workshop performance? | Error rate, safety incidents, job completion time |\n\nA supervisor should at minimum conduct Level 1 (feedback form) and Level 3 (observation) evaluations.\n\n---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. Company training policy\n2. JPK/DSD training guidelines\n3. Manufacturer training catalogues\n4. Kirkpatrick Partners — Four Levels of Training Evaluation","originalFile":"# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KP(3/4)\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** PUTIH / WHITE\n\n---\n\n## Work Activity 3: Coordinate BEV Subordinate's Training\n\n## Objektif Pembelajaran\n1. Explain Training Needs Identification (TNI) for BEV technicians\n2. Describe Training Needs Analysis (TNA) methodology\n3. Explain the process for arranging subordinate training\n\n---\n\n## 1. BEV Training Needs Identification (TNI) — Pengenalpastian Keperluan Latihan\n\n### 1.1 BEV-Related Training Programs\nThe rapid evolution of BEV technology requires continuous training. Key training areas:\n- HV safety certification and recertification\n- New BEV model familiarisation\n- Diagnostic tool software updates and new features\n- Battery technology advances (solid-state, LFP, NMC variants)\n- ADAS (Advanced Driver Assistance Systems) integration with BEV\n- Environmental regulations updates\n\n### 1.2 Skills Chart (Carta Kemahiran)\nMaintain a skills chart for each technician mapping:\n- Current certifications held (NOSS levels, manufacturer certifications)\n- Skills demonstrated (HV work, diagnostics, specific component replacement)\n- Skills gaps identified\n- Training completed and planned\n\n### 1.3 BEV Service Information Updates\nManufacturers regularly release:\n- Technical Service Bulletins (TSBs) requiring new knowledge\n- Safety recalls requiring specific training\n- New model introduction training\n- Updated service procedures\n\n---\n\n## 2. BEV Training Needs Analysis (TNA) — Analisis Keperluan Latihan\n\n### 2.1 Skills Matrix Chart\nA skills matrix maps each technician against required competencies:\n\n| Competency | Tech A | Tech B | Tech C |\n|-----------|--------|--------|--------|\n| HV de-energisation | Y | Y | N |\n| Battery pack removal | Y | N | N |\n| OBC replacement | Y | Y | N |\n| Inverter replacement | N | N | N |\n| HVAC system service | Y | Y | Y |\n| Brake system service | Y | Y | N |\n\nY = Competent, N = Training needed\n\n### 2.2 TNA Form\nThe TNA form documents:\n- Current competency level per technician\n- Required competency level for their role\n- Gap between current and required\n- Priority ranking (critical vs. desirable)\n- Recommended training intervention\n\n### 2.3 Training Program Availability\nSurvey available training:\n- JPK-accredited training centres (Pusat Bertauliah)\n- Manufacturer training academies (e.g., Tesla, BYD, Mercedes training centres)\n- Industry associations (MAA, PEKEMA)\n- Online/e-learning platforms\n- In-house training by senior staff\n\n---\n\n## 3. Arrange Subordinate's Training (Pengurusan Latihan)\n\n### 3.1 Training Plan (TP)\nDevelop an annual training plan:\n- List all required training for each technician\n- Schedule by quarter to minimise operational disruption\n- Budget allocation per training\n- Approval process (supervisor, management)\n\n### 3.2 Training Logistics\n| Item | Action |\n|------|--------|\n| Training location | Confirm venue (in-house, external centre, online) |\n| Training provider | Confirm accredited provider, instructor qualifications |\n| Training notification | Issue notification to trainee minimum 2 weeks in advance |\n| Work coverage | Arrange work coverage during trainee's absence |\n| Materials and facilities | Confirm training materials, tools, equipment availability |\n\n### 3.3 Post-Training\n- Collect training certificate/completion record\n- Update skills chart and personnel file\n- Evaluate training effectiveness (can the technician now perform the skill?)\n- Share key learnings with team during daily briefing\n\n---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. Company training policy\n2. JPK/DSD training guidelines\n3. Manufacturer training catalogues\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":99,"oldLines":9,"newStart":99,"newLines":22,"lines":[" - Evaluate training effectiveness (can the technician now perform the skill?)"," - Share key learnings with team during daily briefing"," ","+### 3.4 Training Effectiveness Evaluation — Kirkpatrick Model","+Evaluate training at four levels:","+","+| Level | What to Evaluate | How |","+|-------|-----------------|-----|","+| **1 — Reaction (Reaksi)** | Did the trainee find the training relevant and satisfactory? | Post-training feedback form |","+| **2 — Learning (Pembelajaran)** | Did the trainee acquire the knowledge/skill? | Post-training test or practical assessment |","+| **3 — Behaviour (Tingkah Laku)** | Is the trainee applying new skills on the job? | Supervisor observation after 30/60 days |","+| **4 — Results (Keputusan)** | Did training improve workshop performance? | Error rate, safety incidents, job completion time |","+","+A supervisor should at minimum conduct Level 1 (feedback form) and Level 3 (observation) evaluations.","+"," ---"," "," ## Rujukan"," 1. Company training policy"," 2. JPK/DSD training guidelines"," 3. Manufacturer training catalogues","+4. Kirkpatrick Partners — Four Levels of Training Evaluation"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01FfFTbwkPpn9r74es9oWfX3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014QvsxKTPkUiNFA3dsPZzNP","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-04.md","old_string":"### 3.3 Reporting to Superior\n- Compile SHE findings into a periodic report (weekly or monthly)\n- Highlight critical and high-risk findings requiring immediate attention\n- Track corrective action completion status\n- Submit report to superior per company procedure\n\n---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. OSHA 1994 (Act 514)\n2. Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127)\n3. Company SHE policies and procedures\n4. DOSH Guidelines for Occupational Safety and Health at Workplace","new_string":"### 3.3 Reporting to Superior\n- Compile SHE findings into a periodic report (weekly or monthly)\n- Highlight critical and high-risk findings requiring immediate attention\n- Track corrective action completion status\n- Submit report to superior per company procedure\n\n---\n\n## 4. Additional SHE Supervisory Requirements\n\n### 4.1 Chemical Health Risk Assessment (CHRA)\nBEV workshops handle scheduled chemicals (coolants, refrigerants, cleaning solvents). The supervisor must:\n- Maintain an updated Chemical Register (Daftar Bahan Kimia) listing all chemicals in use\n- Ensure Safety Data Sheets (SDS/MSDS) are accessible at point of use\n- Monitor compliance with CHRA requirements under Occupational Safety and Health (Use and Standards of Exposure of Chemicals Hazardous to Health) Regulations 2000\n- Review CHRA report and implement recommended controls\n\n### 4.2 Emergency Contact List\nDisplay and maintain a current emergency contact list in the workshop including:\n- BOMBA (Fire Department): 994\n- Police: 999\n- Ambulance: 999\n- Tenaga Nasional Berhad (TNB) electrical emergency: 15454\n- Company safety officer (with direct number)\n- Nearest hospital (with address)\n\nReview and update the emergency contact list quarterly.\n\n---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. OSHA 1994 (Act 514)\n2. Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127)\n3. Company SHE policies and procedures\n4. DOSH Guidelines for Occupational Safety and Health at Workplace\n5. OSH (Use and Standards of Exposure of Chemicals Hazardous to Health) Regulations 2000"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":604,"cache_read_input_tokens":47437,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":604},"output_tokens":63,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"2d9d015e-d41e-4b78-9639-ba4753ddcc59"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014QvsxKTPkUiNFA3dsPZzNP","type":"tool_result","content":"The file C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-04.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"bf1f2bf6-5425-403c-afa4-bfc3ef3fc3dc","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:20:25.423Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KP-04.md","oldString":"### 3.3 Reporting to Superior\n- Compile SHE findings into a periodic report (weekly or monthly)\n- Highlight critical and high-risk findings requiring immediate attention\n- Track corrective action completion status\n- Submit report to superior per company procedure\n\n---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. OSHA 1994 (Act 514)\n2. Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127)\n3. Company SHE policies and procedures\n4. DOSH Guidelines for Occupational Safety and Health at Workplace","newString":"### 3.3 Reporting to Superior\n- Compile SHE findings into a periodic report (weekly or monthly)\n- Highlight critical and high-risk findings requiring immediate attention\n- Track corrective action completion status\n- Submit report to superior per company procedure\n\n---\n\n## 4. Additional SHE Supervisory Requirements\n\n### 4.1 Chemical Health Risk Assessment (CHRA)\nBEV workshops handle scheduled chemicals (coolants, refrigerants, cleaning solvents). The supervisor must:\n- Maintain an updated Chemical Register (Daftar Bahan Kimia) listing all chemicals in use\n- Ensure Safety Data Sheets (SDS/MSDS) are accessible at point of use\n- Monitor compliance with CHRA requirements under Occupational Safety and Health (Use and Standards of Exposure of Chemicals Hazardous to Health) Regulations 2000\n- Review CHRA report and implement recommended controls\n\n### 4.2 Emergency Contact List\nDisplay and maintain a current emergency contact list in the workshop including:\n- BOMBA (Fire Department): 994\n- Police: 999\n- Ambulance: 999\n- Tenaga Nasional Berhad (TNB) electrical emergency: 15454\n- Company safety officer (with direct number)\n- Nearest hospital (with address)\n\nReview and update the emergency contact list quarterly.\n\n---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. OSHA 1994 (Act 514)\n2. Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127)\n3. Company SHE policies and procedures\n4. DOSH Guidelines for Occupational Safety and Health at Workplace\n5. OSH (Use and Standards of Exposure of Chemicals Hazardous to Health) Regulations 2000","originalFile":"# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KP(4/4)\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** PUTIH / WHITE\n\n---\n\n## Work Activity 4: Monitor BEV Workshop Safety, Health & Environment\n\n## Objektif Pembelajaran\n1. Explain BEV workshop safety, health, and environment responsibilities\n2. Describe BEV-specific safety, health, and environment awareness requirements\n3. Explain the SHE finding report format and reporting process\n\n---\n\n## 1. BEV Safety, Health, and Environment (Keselamatan, Kesihatan dan Alam Sekitar)\n\n### 1.1 Employer and Employee Responsibilities\n\n**Employer responsibilities (under OSHA 1994):**\n- Provide a safe workplace with appropriate HV safety equipment\n- Ensure all BEV technicians are properly trained and certified\n- Maintain safety equipment in serviceable condition\n- Provide PPE at no cost to employees\n- Establish and enforce safety procedures\n- Report workplace accidents and dangerous occurrences\n\n**Employee responsibilities:**\n- Follow established safety procedures\n- Wear prescribed PPE at all times\n- Report hazards, incidents, and near-misses immediately\n- Not tamper with or disable safety equipment\n- Participate in safety training and briefings\n\n### 1.2 Workplace Safety and Health\nThe supervisor must monitor:\n- HV safety procedures are followed for every BEV job\n- PPE is worn correctly and consistently\n- Tools and equipment are in serviceable condition\n- Work permits are obtained for HV work\n- Two-person rule is observed for HV work\n- Emergency equipment is accessible and maintained\n\n### 1.3 Waste Management Monitoring\nThe supervisor ensures:\n- Scheduled waste is properly classified, contained, and labeled\n- Waste disposal records are maintained\n- Licensed waste contractors are used\n- DOE reporting requirements are met\n- Waste storage areas are compliant\n\n---\n\n## 2. BEV Safety, Health, and Environment Awareness\n\n### 2.1 Basics of Safety, Health, and Environment\n\n**Proper BEV PPE:**\n- Monitor that all technicians have complete, inspected PPE\n- Verify insulated gloves are tested before each use\n- Replace damaged PPE immediately\n\n**Workplace Hazard Awareness:**\n- Conduct regular hazard inspections (daily walk-through)\n- Verify HV warning signage is in place\n- Check that barriers are used during HV work\n- Ensure fire extinguishers are accessible and current\n\n**Accident Prevention:**\n- Review and update SOPs regularly\n- Investigate all incidents and near-misses\n- Implement corrective actions from investigations\n- Share lessons learned during daily briefings\n\n### 2.2 Waste Disposal Procedure Monitoring\n- Verify proper storage, segregation, tagging, and signage of waste\n- Monitor waste inventory logs\n- Ensure timely pickup by licensed contractors\n\n### 2.3 Workshop Best Practice\n**Do's:**\n- Always de-energise HV before physical work\n- Always verify zero-energy state with multimeter\n- Always wear full PPE in BEV work bay\n- Always use insulated tools for HV-adjacent work\n- Always maintain two-person rule for HV work\n\n**Don'ts:**\n- Never assume HV is disconnected — always verify\n- Never wear metallic jewellery during HV work\n- Never skip the capacitor discharge waiting period\n- Never use damaged PPE or tools\n- Never work alone on HV systems\n\n### 2.4 Emergency Response\nThe supervisor ensures all personnel are trained in:\n- **First aid:** Basic first aid, CPR, AED operation\n- **Firefighting:** Class D fire extinguisher operation for lithium battery fires\n- **Electric shock response:** Use of rescue hook, disconnection procedures, CPR/AED\n- **Thermal runaway response:** Evacuation procedure, Class D extinguisher, BOMBA notification\n\n---\n\n## 3. SHE Finding Report\n\n### 3.1 Report Format and Contents\n\n| Section | Content |\n|---------|---------|\n| Date and time | When the finding was observed |\n| Location | Specific area within the workshop |\n| Finding description | What was observed (hazard, non-compliance, positive observation) |\n| Photo evidence | Photos of the finding |\n| Risk assessment | Severity and likelihood rating |\n| Recommendation | Corrective action proposed |\n| Responsible person | Who should action the recommendation |\n| Target date | When the corrective action should be completed |\n| Follow-up | Verification that action was taken |\n\n### 3.2 Risk Assessment Report\nEach SHE finding should include a risk assessment:\n- **Severity:** Minor / Moderate / Major / Critical\n- **Likelihood:** Unlikely / Possible / Likely / Almost certain\n- **Risk rating:** Low / Medium / High / Extreme\n- **Photo documentation** of the finding\n- **Date, time, and location** recorded\n- **Recommendation** for corrective action\n\n### 3.3 Reporting to Superior\n- Compile SHE findings into a periodic report (weekly or monthly)\n- Highlight critical and high-risk findings requiring immediate attention\n- Track corrective action completion status\n- Submit report to superior per company procedure\n\n---\n\n## Rujukan\n1. OSHA 1994 (Act 514)\n2. Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127)\n3. Company SHE policies and procedures\n4. DOSH Guidelines for Occupational Safety and Health at Workplace\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":135,"oldLines":8,"newStart":135,"newLines":31,"lines":[" "," ---"," ","+## 4. Additional SHE Supervisory Requirements","+","+### 4.1 Chemical Health Risk Assessment (CHRA)","+BEV workshops handle scheduled chemicals (coolants, refrigerants, cleaning solvents). The supervisor must:","+- Maintain an updated Chemical Register (Daftar Bahan Kimia) listing all chemicals in use","+- Ensure Safety Data Sheets (SDS/MSDS) are accessible at point of use","+- Monitor compliance with CHRA requirements under Occupational Safety and Health (Use and Standards of Exposure of Chemicals Hazardous to Health) Regulations 2000","+- Review CHRA report and implement recommended controls","+","+### 4.2 Emergency Contact List","+Display and maintain a current emergency contact list in the workshop including:","+- BOMBA (Fire Department): 994","+- Police: 999","+- Ambulance: 999","+- Tenaga Nasional Berhad (TNB) electrical emergency: 15454","+- Company safety officer (with direct number)","+- Nearest hospital (with address)","+","+Review and update the emergency contact list quarterly.","+","+---","+"," ## Rujukan"," 1. OSHA 1994 (Act 514)"," 2. Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127)"," 3. Company SHE policies and procedures"," 4. DOSH Guidelines for Occupational Safety and Health at Workplace","+5. OSH (Use and Standards of Exposure of Chemicals Hazardous to Health) Regulations 2000"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01RjycchpX2bsuond1CJJCbq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now enhance the KT files with additional varied question formats:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":726,"cache_read_input_tokens":48041,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":726},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"21284a95-b00c-4f88-a0e8-501c0f43b3d3"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01RjycchpX2bsuond1CJJCbq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VseLbRof5CgA9jvewigaBP","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KT-01.md","old_string":"### S5 — Short Answer: Describe the role of the job control board in workshop management. What information should it display?\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** Safety reminder, work progress update, new assignments, service information updates, housekeeping/5S, questions and feedback.\n**S2:** B\n**S3:** a) FALSE — mandatory, auditable document b) TRUE c) TRUE\n**S4:** Clarity (simple/direct language), brevity (concise), structure (consistent format), engagement (questions/input), visual aids (whiteboard/job board). Any three.\n**S5:** Displays: vehicle registration and job status, technician assignments, expected completion times, parts status. Updated daily to track workshop workflow.","new_string":"### S5 — Short Answer: Describe the role of the job control board in workshop management. What information should it display?\n\n### S6 — Case Study: Ali is a newly promoted BEV workshop supervisor. During his first daily briefing, two senior technicians dominate the discussion while junior technicians remain silent. After the briefing, a junior technician privately tells Ali that she has a concern about a vehicle's HV warning light but was afraid to speak up. (a) Which leadership style should Ali apply in his next briefing to encourage participation from all team members? (b) State TWO specific actions Ali can take to ensure all team members can raise safety concerns during briefings.\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** Safety reminder, work progress update, new assignments, service information updates, housekeeping/5S, questions and feedback.\n**S2:** B\n**S3:** a) FALSE — mandatory, auditable document b) TRUE c) TRUE\n**S4:** Clarity (simple/direct language), brevity (concise), structure (consistent format), engagement (questions/input), visual aids (whiteboard/job board). Any three.\n**S5:** Displays: vehicle registration and job status, technician assignments, expected completion times, parts status. Updated daily to track workshop workflow.\n**S6:** a) Democratic or coaching style — consult team members, invite input, create a safe environment for questions. b) Any two: create structured Q&A round at end of briefing; use anonymous written feedback cards; acknowledge all concerns without judgement; establish \"no such thing as a stupid safety question\" policy; speak to quieter members individually."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":726,"cache_read_input_tokens":48041,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":726},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"687c1fc3-d607-4925-96a4-cb5f8320acc7"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VseLbRof5CgA9jvewigaBP","type":"tool_result","content":"The file C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KT-01.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"e5d9944d-7036-4d15-b4e2-92e40956a2d4","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:20:40.840Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KT-01.md","oldString":"### S5 — Short Answer: Describe the role of the job control board in workshop management. What information should it display?\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** Safety reminder, work progress update, new assignments, service information updates, housekeeping/5S, questions and feedback.\n**S2:** B\n**S3:** a) FALSE — mandatory, auditable document b) TRUE c) TRUE\n**S4:** Clarity (simple/direct language), brevity (concise), structure (consistent format), engagement (questions/input), visual aids (whiteboard/job board). Any three.\n**S5:** Displays: vehicle registration and job status, technician assignments, expected completion times, parts status. Updated daily to track workshop workflow.","newString":"### S5 — Short Answer: Describe the role of the job control board in workshop management. What information should it display?\n\n### S6 — Case Study: Ali is a newly promoted BEV workshop supervisor. During his first daily briefing, two senior technicians dominate the discussion while junior technicians remain silent. After the briefing, a junior technician privately tells Ali that she has a concern about a vehicle's HV warning light but was afraid to speak up. (a) Which leadership style should Ali apply in his next briefing to encourage participation from all team members? (b) State TWO specific actions Ali can take to ensure all team members can raise safety concerns during briefings.\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** Safety reminder, work progress update, new assignments, service information updates, housekeeping/5S, questions and feedback.\n**S2:** B\n**S3:** a) FALSE — mandatory, auditable document b) TRUE c) TRUE\n**S4:** Clarity (simple/direct language), brevity (concise), structure (consistent format), engagement (questions/input), visual aids (whiteboard/job board). Any three.\n**S5:** Displays: vehicle registration and job status, technician assignments, expected completion times, parts status. Updated daily to track workshop workflow.\n**S6:** a) Democratic or coaching style — consult team members, invite input, create a safe environment for questions. b) Any two: create structured Q&A round at end of briefing; use anonymous written feedback cards; acknowledge all concerns without judgement; establish \"no such thing as a stupid safety question\" policy; speak to quieter members individually.","originalFile":"# KERTAS TUGASAN / ASSIGNMENT SHEET\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KT(1/4)\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** MERAH JAMBU / PINK\n\n## Work Activity 1: Conduct BEV Daily Briefing | **Reference:** C05/KP(1/4)\n\n### S1 — Short Answer: List SIX (6) items that should be covered in a BEV workshop daily briefing.\n### S2 — MCQ: What is the recommended maximum duration for a daily briefing? A. 5 minutes B. 15-20 minutes C. 45 minutes D. 1 hour\n### S3 — T/F: a) Attendance list is optional for daily briefings. b) The job control board should be updated after the briefing. c) Safety concerns raised during briefing should be documented.\n### S4 — Short Answer: Explain THREE (3) effective communication techniques for conducting a daily briefing.\n### S5 — Short Answer: Describe the role of the job control board in workshop management. What information should it display?\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** Safety reminder, work progress update, new assignments, service information updates, housekeeping/5S, questions and feedback.\n**S2:** B\n**S3:** a) FALSE — mandatory, auditable document b) TRUE c) TRUE\n**S4:** Clarity (simple/direct language), brevity (concise), structure (consistent format), engagement (questions/input), visual aids (whiteboard/job board). Any three.\n**S5:** Displays: vehicle registration and job status, technician assignments, expected completion times, parts status. Updated daily to track workshop workflow.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":11,"oldLines":9,"newStart":11,"newLines":12,"lines":[" ### S4 — Short Answer: Explain THREE (3) effective communication techniques for conducting a daily briefing."," ### S5 — Short Answer: Describe the role of the job control board in workshop management. What information should it display?"," ","+### S6 — Case Study: Ali is a newly promoted BEV workshop supervisor. During his first daily briefing, two senior technicians dominate the discussion while junior technicians remain silent. After the briefing, a junior technician privately tells Ali that she has a concern about a vehicle's HV warning light but was afraid to speak up. (a) Which leadership style should Ali apply in his next briefing to encourage participation from all team members? (b) State TWO specific actions Ali can take to ensure all team members can raise safety concerns during briefings.","+"," ## Skema Jawapan"," **S1:** Safety reminder, work progress update, new assignments, service information updates, housekeeping/5S, questions and feedback."," **S2:** B"," **S3:** a) FALSE — mandatory, auditable document b) TRUE c) TRUE"," **S4:** Clarity (simple/direct language), brevity (concise), structure (consistent format), engagement (questions/input), visual aids (whiteboard/job board). Any three."," **S5:** Displays: vehicle registration and job status, technician assignments, expected completion times, parts status. Updated daily to track workshop workflow.","+**S6:** a) Democratic or coaching style — consult team members, invite input, create a safe environment for questions. b) Any two: create structured Q&A round at end of briefing; use anonymous written feedback cards; acknowledge all concerns without judgement; establish \"no such thing as a stupid safety question\" policy; speak to quieter members individually."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Dk4wuhnD3BPjYwMJHXtCQf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013nDPSaiEPG9irHrhFGT5KK","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KT-02.md","old_string":"### S6 — Short Answer: What FOUR (4) improvement techniques can a supervisor use for underperforming technicians?\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** Attitude, skills, knowledge, productivity and efficiency.\n**S2:** B\n**S3:** 1-c, 2-b, 3-a\n**S4:** Can technician independently perform HV de-energisation? Do they consistently follow safety procedures? Can they interpret BEV diagnostic fault codes? Any two.\n**S5:** a) FALSE — formal annually/semi-annually, informal monthly b) TRUE c) TRUE\n**S6:** On-the-Job Training (OJT), refresher course, counselling, mentoring.","new_string":"### S6 — Short Answer: What FOUR (4) improvement techniques can a supervisor use for underperforming technicians?\n\n### S7 — Case Study: Technician Siti consistently scores \"Excellent\" on HV de-energisation and diagnostics but is frequently late and has low attendance. Using the SMART framework, write ONE performance improvement target for Siti's punctuality issue. Include all five SMART elements.\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** Attitude, skills, knowledge, productivity and efficiency.\n**S2:** B\n**S3:** 1-c, 2-b, 3-a\n**S4:** Can technician independently perform HV de-energisation? Do they consistently follow safety procedures? Can they interpret BEV diagnostic fault codes? Any two.\n**S5:** a) FALSE — formal annually/semi-annually, informal monthly b) TRUE c) TRUE\n**S6:** On-the-Job Training (OJT), refresher course, counselling, mentoring.\n**S7:** Example: S — Arrive at workshop by 7:45 AM (before 8:00 AM briefing start); M — Zero late arrivals in 30 consecutive working days; A — Achievable with a change in personal routine; R — Punctuality directly impacts team briefing effectiveness and customer vehicle readiness; T — Reviewed at one-month follow-up counselling session. (Accept any answer that correctly addresses all five SMART elements with a logical connection to punctuality/attendance.)"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":745,"cache_read_input_tokens":48767,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":745},"output_tokens":63,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"c072278e-4ec8-4733-8559-d70c59bfa826"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013nDPSaiEPG9irHrhFGT5KK","type":"tool_result","content":"The file C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KT-02.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"59d9af9f-b0c9-43e2-a089-abaaefb57eeb","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:20:55.081Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KT-02.md","oldString":"### S6 — Short Answer: What FOUR (4) improvement techniques can a supervisor use for underperforming technicians?\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** Attitude, skills, knowledge, productivity and efficiency.\n**S2:** B\n**S3:** 1-c, 2-b, 3-a\n**S4:** Can technician independently perform HV de-energisation? Do they consistently follow safety procedures? Can they interpret BEV diagnostic fault codes? Any two.\n**S5:** a) FALSE — formal annually/semi-annually, informal monthly b) TRUE c) TRUE\n**S6:** On-the-Job Training (OJT), refresher course, counselling, mentoring.","newString":"### S6 — Short Answer: What FOUR (4) improvement techniques can a supervisor use for underperforming technicians?\n\n### S7 — Case Study: Technician Siti consistently scores \"Excellent\" on HV de-energisation and diagnostics but is frequently late and has low attendance. Using the SMART framework, write ONE performance improvement target for Siti's punctuality issue. Include all five SMART elements.\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** Attitude, skills, knowledge, productivity and efficiency.\n**S2:** B\n**S3:** 1-c, 2-b, 3-a\n**S4:** Can technician independently perform HV de-energisation? Do they consistently follow safety procedures? Can they interpret BEV diagnostic fault codes? Any two.\n**S5:** a) FALSE — formal annually/semi-annually, informal monthly b) TRUE c) TRUE\n**S6:** On-the-Job Training (OJT), refresher course, counselling, mentoring.\n**S7:** Example: S — Arrive at workshop by 7:45 AM (before 8:00 AM briefing start); M — Zero late arrivals in 30 consecutive working days; A — Achievable with a change in personal routine; R — Punctuality directly impacts team briefing effectiveness and customer vehicle readiness; T — Reviewed at one-month follow-up counselling session. (Accept any answer that correctly addresses all five SMART elements with a logical connection to punctuality/attendance.)","originalFile":"# KERTAS TUGASAN / ASSIGNMENT SHEET\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KT(2/4)\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** MERAH JAMBU / PINK\n\n## Work Activity 2: Monitor BEV Subordinate's Performance | **Reference:** C05/KP(2/4)\n\n### S1 — Short Answer: List FOUR (4) evaluation dimensions used to assess BEV technician performance.\n### S2 — MCQ: Which is a performance improvement technique for skill gaps? A. Salary increase B. On-the-Job Training (OJT) C. Vacation D. Transfer\n### S3 — Matching: 1. Evaluation form 2. Counselling form 3. Training nomination form (a. Recommend training for skill gaps b. Document counselling sessions c. Formal performance rating)\n### S4 — Short Answer: Describe TWO (2) BEV-specific competency indicators a supervisor should assess.\n### S5 — T/F: a) Performance appraisal is only done annually. b) Counselling is used for persistent performance problems. c) Performance reports are confidential documents.\n### S6 — Short Answer: What FOUR (4) improvement techniques can a supervisor use for underperforming technicians?\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** Attitude, skills, knowledge, productivity and efficiency.\n**S2:** B\n**S3:** 1-c, 2-b, 3-a\n**S4:** Can technician independently perform HV de-energisation? Do they consistently follow safety procedures? Can they interpret BEV diagnostic fault codes? Any two.\n**S5:** a) FALSE — formal annually/semi-annually, informal monthly b) TRUE c) TRUE\n**S6:** On-the-Job Training (OJT), refresher course, counselling, mentoring.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":12,"oldLines":6,"newStart":12,"newLines":8,"lines":[" ### S5 — T/F: a) Performance appraisal is only done annually. b) Counselling is used for persistent performance problems. c) Performance reports are confidential documents."," ### S6 — Short Answer: What FOUR (4) improvement techniques can a supervisor use for underperforming technicians?"," ","+### S7 — Case Study: Technician Siti consistently scores \"Excellent\" on HV de-energisation and diagnostics but is frequently late and has low attendance. Using the SMART framework, write ONE performance improvement target for Siti's punctuality issue. Include all five SMART elements.","+"," ## Skema Jawapan"," **S1:** Attitude, skills, knowledge, productivity and efficiency."," **S2:** B"]},{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":3,"newStart":21,"newLines":4,"lines":[" **S4:** Can technician independently perform HV de-energisation? Do they consistently follow safety procedures? Can they interpret BEV diagnostic fault codes? Any two."," **S5:** a) FALSE — formal annually/semi-annually, informal monthly b) TRUE c) TRUE"," **S6:** On-the-Job Training (OJT), refresher course, counselling, mentoring.","+**S7:** Example: S — Arrive at workshop by 7:45 AM (before 8:00 AM briefing start); M — Zero late arrivals in 30 consecutive working days; A — Achievable with a change in personal routine; R — Punctuality directly impacts team briefing effectiveness and customer vehicle readiness; T — Reviewed at one-month follow-up counselling session. (Accept any answer that correctly addresses all five SMART elements with a logical connection to punctuality/attendance.)"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01U3ev1vdUtpZMHos8QJr7u7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MTC3GcaufXJUFoQRknYBR4","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KT-03.md","old_string":"### S5 — Short Answer: List FIVE (5) training logistics items that must be arranged when coordinating subordinate training.\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** a) TNI: identifying what training is needed based on skill gaps, business needs, new technology. b) TNA: analysing the gap between current and required competency levels, then prioritising and selecting appropriate training interventions. TNI identifies needs; TNA analyses and prioritises them.\n**S2:** Example matrix with Y/N entries showing skill gaps; training recommended where N. (Accept any reasonable matrix.)\n**S3:** C\n**S4:** a) TRUE b) FALSE — minimum 2 weeks advance notice c) TRUE\n**S5:** Training location, training provider, training notification (2 weeks), work coverage, materials and facilities.","new_string":"### S5 — Short Answer: List FIVE (5) training logistics items that must be arranged when coordinating subordinate training.\n\n### S6 — Case Study: After sending Technician Kumar to a 3-day HV battery diagnostic course, his supervisor notices that Kumar still asks colleagues for help when using the new diagnostic tool two months later. (a) At which Kirkpatrick evaluation level did the training fail? (b) State TWO corrective actions the supervisor should take.\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** a) TNI: identifying what training is needed based on skill gaps, business needs, new technology. b) TNA: analysing the gap between current and required competency levels, then prioritising and selecting appropriate training interventions. TNI identifies needs; TNA analyses and prioritises them.\n**S2:** Example matrix with Y/N entries showing skill gaps; training recommended where N. (Accept any reasonable matrix.)\n**S3:** C\n**S4:** a) TRUE b) FALSE — minimum 2 weeks advance notice c) TRUE\n**S5:** Training location, training provider, training notification (2 weeks), work coverage, materials and facilities.\n**S6:** a) Level 3 — Behaviour (the technician learned in training but is not applying the skills on the job). b) Any two: arrange supervised OJT sessions with the diagnostic tool; pair Kumar with a senior technician as mentor; check if Kumar needs additional practice time with the equipment; conduct a Level 2 re-assessment to verify actual skill level; set a SMART target for independent tool use within 30 days."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":713,"cache_read_input_tokens":49512,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":713},"output_tokens":63,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"f8fac656-0a2b-491b-8021-4cd70d6d43e9"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MTC3GcaufXJUFoQRknYBR4","type":"tool_result","content":"The file C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KT-03.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"b2c9a5ad-d9b6-436b-9254-2b4a7b4b4c9c","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:21:10.447Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KT-03.md","oldString":"### S5 — Short Answer: List FIVE (5) training logistics items that must be arranged when coordinating subordinate training.\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** a) TNI: identifying what training is needed based on skill gaps, business needs, new technology. b) TNA: analysing the gap between current and required competency levels, then prioritising and selecting appropriate training interventions. TNI identifies needs; TNA analyses and prioritises them.\n**S2:** Example matrix with Y/N entries showing skill gaps; training recommended where N. (Accept any reasonable matrix.)\n**S3:** C\n**S4:** a) TRUE b) FALSE — minimum 2 weeks advance notice c) TRUE\n**S5:** Training location, training provider, training notification (2 weeks), work coverage, materials and facilities.","newString":"### S5 — Short Answer: List FIVE (5) training logistics items that must be arranged when coordinating subordinate training.\n\n### S6 — Case Study: After sending Technician Kumar to a 3-day HV battery diagnostic course, his supervisor notices that Kumar still asks colleagues for help when using the new diagnostic tool two months later. (a) At which Kirkpatrick evaluation level did the training fail? (b) State TWO corrective actions the supervisor should take.\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** a) TNI: identifying what training is needed based on skill gaps, business needs, new technology. b) TNA: analysing the gap between current and required competency levels, then prioritising and selecting appropriate training interventions. TNI identifies needs; TNA analyses and prioritises them.\n**S2:** Example matrix with Y/N entries showing skill gaps; training recommended where N. (Accept any reasonable matrix.)\n**S3:** C\n**S4:** a) TRUE b) FALSE — minimum 2 weeks advance notice c) TRUE\n**S5:** Training location, training provider, training notification (2 weeks), work coverage, materials and facilities.\n**S6:** a) Level 3 — Behaviour (the technician learned in training but is not applying the skills on the job). b) Any two: arrange supervised OJT sessions with the diagnostic tool; pair Kumar with a senior technician as mentor; check if Kumar needs additional practice time with the equipment; conduct a Level 2 re-assessment to verify actual skill level; set a SMART target for independent tool use within 30 days.","originalFile":"# KERTAS TUGASAN / ASSIGNMENT SHEET\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KT(3/4)\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** MERAH JAMBU / PINK\n\n## Work Activity 3: Coordinate BEV Subordinate's Training | **Reference:** C05/KP(3/4)\n\n### S1 — Short Answer: a) What is TNI (Training Needs Identification)? b) What is TNA (Training Needs Analysis)? Explain how they differ.\n### S2 — Short Answer: Create a simple skills matrix for 3 technicians and 4 BEV competencies. Identify which technician needs what training.\n### S3 — MCQ: Which is NOT a source for BEV training? A. JPK-accredited centres B. Manufacturer training academies C. Social media influencers D. Industry associations\n### S4 — T/F: a) A skills chart maps each technician against required competencies. b) Training notification should be given on the day of training. c) Post-training evaluation includes checking if the technician can now perform the skill.\n### S5 — Short Answer: List FIVE (5) training logistics items that must be arranged when coordinating subordinate training.\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** a) TNI: identifying what training is needed based on skill gaps, business needs, new technology. b) TNA: analysing the gap between current and required competency levels, then prioritising and selecting appropriate training interventions. TNI identifies needs; TNA analyses and prioritises them.\n**S2:** Example matrix with Y/N entries showing skill gaps; training recommended where N. (Accept any reasonable matrix.)\n**S3:** C\n**S4:** a) TRUE b) FALSE — minimum 2 weeks advance notice c) TRUE\n**S5:** Training location, training provider, training notification (2 weeks), work coverage, materials and facilities.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":11,"oldLines":9,"newStart":11,"newLines":12,"lines":[" ### S4 — T/F: a) A skills chart maps each technician against required competencies. b) Training notification should be given on the day of training. c) Post-training evaluation includes checking if the technician can now perform the skill."," ### S5 — Short Answer: List FIVE (5) training logistics items that must be arranged when coordinating subordinate training."," ","+### S6 — Case Study: After sending Technician Kumar to a 3-day HV battery diagnostic course, his supervisor notices that Kumar still asks colleagues for help when using the new diagnostic tool two months later. (a) At which Kirkpatrick evaluation level did the training fail? (b) State TWO corrective actions the supervisor should take.","+"," ## Skema Jawapan"," **S1:** a) TNI: identifying what training is needed based on skill gaps, business needs, new technology. b) TNA: analysing the gap between current and required competency levels, then prioritising and selecting appropriate training interventions. TNI identifies needs; TNA analyses and prioritises them."," **S2:** Example matrix with Y/N entries showing skill gaps; training recommended where N. (Accept any reasonable matrix.)"," **S3:** C"," **S4:** a) TRUE b) FALSE — minimum 2 weeks advance notice c) TRUE"," **S5:** Training location, training provider, training notification (2 weeks), work coverage, materials and facilities.","+**S6:** a) Level 3 — Behaviour (the technician learned in training but is not applying the skills on the job). b) Any two: arrange supervised OJT sessions with the diagnostic tool; pair Kumar with a senior technician as mentor; check if Kumar needs additional practice time with the equipment; conduct a Level 2 re-assessment to verify actual skill level; set a SMART target for independent tool use within 30 days."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01W9GQKHPSN5nBiQLy1YHgy7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WReQCLZ2Pxt3JiGVKPyLL5","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KT-04.md","old_string":"### S6 — Short Answer: Explain the risk assessment process for a SHE finding. Include severity, likelihood, and risk rating.\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** Employer: provide safe workplace, train/certify staff, maintain safety equipment, provide PPE. Employee: follow safety procedures, wear PPE, report hazards, participate in training.\n**S2:** C\n**S3:** Do's: de-energise HV, verify zero energy, wear PPE, use insulated tools, maintain two-person rule. Don'ts: assume HV disconnected, wear jewellery, skip discharge waiting, use damaged PPE, work alone on HV.\n**S4:** Date/time, location, finding description, photo evidence, risk assessment, recommendation, responsible person, target date, follow-up.\n**S5:** a) FALSE — monthly or per company schedule b) FALSE — mandatory to report c) TRUE\n**S6:** Severity (minor to critical), likelihood (unlikely to almost certain), risk rating (low to extreme) = severity x likelihood. Photo, date, recommendation included.","new_string":"### S6 — Short Answer: Explain the risk assessment process for a SHE finding. Include severity, likelihood, and risk rating.\n\n### S7 — Case Study: During a routine SHE walk-through, Supervisor Lim finds: (i) A Class D fire extinguisher at Bay 3 has an expired service tag (last serviced 14 months ago). (ii) Two technicians are working on an HV battery pack without an insulated mat. (iii) The scheduled waste storage area has an unlabelled container. For EACH finding, state: (a) the risk level (Low / Medium / High / Extreme) and (b) the corrective action and urgency.\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** Employer: provide safe workplace, train/certify staff, maintain safety equipment, provide PPE. Employee: follow safety procedures, wear PPE, report hazards, participate in training.\n**S2:** C\n**S3:** Do's: de-energise HV, verify zero energy, wear PPE, use insulated tools, maintain two-person rule. Don'ts: assume HV disconnected, wear jewellery, skip discharge waiting, use damaged PPE, work alone on HV.\n**S4:** Date/time, location, finding description, photo evidence, risk assessment, recommendation, responsible person, target date, follow-up.\n**S5:** a) FALSE — monthly or per company schedule b) FALSE — mandatory to report c) TRUE\n**S6:** Severity (minor to critical), likelihood (unlikely to almost certain), risk rating (low to extreme) = severity x likelihood. Photo, date, recommendation included.\n**S7:** (i) Expired extinguisher — High risk; Immediate: remove from service, arrange emergency re-servicing or replacement, do not start any HV work at Bay 3 until resolved. (ii) No insulated mat — Extreme risk; Immediate: STOP WORK immediately, deploy insulated mat before resuming, counsel technicians on procedure, document the near-miss. (iii) Unlabelled waste container — Medium risk; Same day: identify and label the container per waste classification, review waste handling procedure with staff, update waste inventory log."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":744,"cache_read_input_tokens":50225,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":744},"output_tokens":63,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"5c602628-c48a-4f3c-8d9d-1125d6651485"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WReQCLZ2Pxt3JiGVKPyLL5","type":"tool_result","content":"The file C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KT-04.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b7e541-fc46-443f-9329-800f1caa525b","uuid":"7e2f0dfc-4e84-422d-8189-1fa5c770c6ba","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:21:41.638Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C05\\KT-04.md","oldString":"### S6 — Short Answer: Explain the risk assessment process for a SHE finding. Include severity, likelihood, and risk rating.\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** Employer: provide safe workplace, train/certify staff, maintain safety equipment, provide PPE. Employee: follow safety procedures, wear PPE, report hazards, participate in training.\n**S2:** C\n**S3:** Do's: de-energise HV, verify zero energy, wear PPE, use insulated tools, maintain two-person rule. Don'ts: assume HV disconnected, wear jewellery, skip discharge waiting, use damaged PPE, work alone on HV.\n**S4:** Date/time, location, finding description, photo evidence, risk assessment, recommendation, responsible person, target date, follow-up.\n**S5:** a) FALSE — monthly or per company schedule b) FALSE — mandatory to report c) TRUE\n**S6:** Severity (minor to critical), likelihood (unlikely to almost certain), risk rating (low to extreme) = severity x likelihood. Photo, date, recommendation included.","newString":"### S6 — Short Answer: Explain the risk assessment process for a SHE finding. Include severity, likelihood, and risk rating.\n\n### S7 — Case Study: During a routine SHE walk-through, Supervisor Lim finds: (i) A Class D fire extinguisher at Bay 3 has an expired service tag (last serviced 14 months ago). (ii) Two technicians are working on an HV battery pack without an insulated mat. (iii) The scheduled waste storage area has an unlabelled container. For EACH finding, state: (a) the risk level (Low / Medium / High / Extreme) and (b) the corrective action and urgency.\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** Employer: provide safe workplace, train/certify staff, maintain safety equipment, provide PPE. Employee: follow safety procedures, wear PPE, report hazards, participate in training.\n**S2:** C\n**S3:** Do's: de-energise HV, verify zero energy, wear PPE, use insulated tools, maintain two-person rule. Don'ts: assume HV disconnected, wear jewellery, skip discharge waiting, use damaged PPE, work alone on HV.\n**S4:** Date/time, location, finding description, photo evidence, risk assessment, recommendation, responsible person, target date, follow-up.\n**S5:** a) FALSE — monthly or per company schedule b) FALSE — mandatory to report c) TRUE\n**S6:** Severity (minor to critical), likelihood (unlikely to almost certain), risk rating (low to extreme) = severity x likelihood. Photo, date, recommendation included.\n**S7:** (i) Expired extinguisher — High risk; Immediate: remove from service, arrange emergency re-servicing or replacement, do not start any HV work at Bay 3 until resolved. (ii) No insulated mat — Extreme risk; Immediate: STOP WORK immediately, deploy insulated mat before resuming, counsel technicians on procedure, document the near-miss. (iii) Unlabelled waste container — Medium risk; Same day: identify and label the container per waste classification, review waste handling procedure with staff, update waste inventory log.","originalFile":"# KERTAS TUGASAN / ASSIGNMENT SHEET\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C05/KT(4/4)\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** MERAH JAMBU / PINK\n\n## Work Activity 4: Monitor BEV Workshop Safety, Health & Environment | **Reference:** C05/KP(4/4)\n\n### S1 — Short Answer: List FOUR (4) employer responsibilities and FOUR (4) employee responsibilities under OSHA 1994 relevant to a BEV workshop.\n### S2 — MCQ: Which emergency response is specific to BEV workshops? A. Fire drill B. Earthquake drill C. Electric shock response using rescue hook and AED D. Flood evacuation\n### S3 — Short Answer: List FIVE (5) \"Do's\" and FIVE (5) \"Don'ts\" for BEV workshop safety.\n### S4 — Short Answer: Describe the format and contents of a SHE finding report. Include at least SIX (6) sections.\n### S5 — T/F: a) Fire extinguisher checks are done annually only. b) Near-miss reports are optional. c) The supervisor should conduct daily hazard walk-throughs.\n### S6 — Short Answer: Explain the risk assessment process for a SHE finding. Include severity, likelihood, and risk rating.\n\n## Skema Jawapan\n**S1:** Employer: provide safe workplace, train/certify staff, maintain safety equipment, provide PPE. Employee: follow safety procedures, wear PPE, report hazards, participate in training.\n**S2:** C\n**S3:** Do's: de-energise HV, verify zero energy, wear PPE, use insulated tools, maintain two-person rule. Don'ts: assume HV disconnected, wear jewellery, skip discharge waiting, use damaged PPE, work alone on HV.\n**S4:** Date/time, location, finding description, photo evidence, risk assessment, recommendation, responsible person, target date, follow-up.\n**S5:** a) FALSE — monthly or per company schedule b) FALSE — mandatory to report c) TRUE\n**S6:** Severity (minor to critical), likelihood (unlikely to almost certain), risk rating (low to extreme) = severity x likelihood. Photo, date, recommendation included.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":12,"oldLines":6,"newStart":12,"newLines":8,"lines":[" ### S5 — T/F: a) Fire extinguisher checks are done annually only. b) Near-miss reports are optional. c) The supervisor should conduct daily hazard walk-throughs."," ### S6 — Short Answer: Explain the risk assessment process for a SHE finding. Include severity, likelihood, and risk rating."," ","+### S7 — Case Study: During a routine SHE walk-through, Supervisor Lim finds: (i) A Class D fire extinguisher at Bay 3 has an expired service tag (last serviced 14 months ago). (ii) Two technicians are working on an HV battery pack without an insulated mat. (iii) The scheduled waste storage area has an unlabelled container. For EACH finding, state: (a) the risk level (Low / Medium / High / Extreme) and (b) the corrective action and urgency.","+"," ## Skema Jawapan"," **S1:** Employer: provide safe workplace, train/certify staff, maintain safety equipment, provide PPE. Employee: follow safety procedures, wear PPE, report hazards, participate in training."," **S2:** C"]},{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":3,"newStart":21,"newLines":4,"lines":[" **S4:** Date/time, location, finding description, photo evidence, risk assessment, recommendation, responsible person, target date, follow-up."," **S5:** a) FALSE — monthly or per company schedule b) FALSE — mandatory to report c) TRUE"," **S6:** Severity (minor to critical), likelihood (unlikely to almost certain), risk rating (low to extreme) = severity x likelihood. Photo, date, recommendation included.","+**S7:** (i) Expired extinguisher — High risk; Immediate: remove from service, arrange emergency re-servicing or replacement, do not start any HV work at Bay 3 until resolved. (ii) No insulated mat — Extreme risk; Immediate: STOP WORK immediately, deploy insulated mat before resuming, counsel technicians on procedure, document the near-miss. (iii) Unlabelled waste container — Medium risk; Same day: identify and label the container per waste classification, review waste handling procedure with staff, update waste inventory log."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
